(window.RK_DATA = window.RK_DATA || {})["expert-en"] = {"modules":[{"id":"x00","title":"Welcome to the workshop","sub":"How to learn here, home assignments and a warm-up","lessons":[{"id":"x00-1","title":"How Level 3 works","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The workshop","md":"Level 1 taught you to drive. Level 2 opened the hood. Level 3 is the **workshop**: here you make your own parts.\n\nWhat changed:\n- **More practice than theory.** Every lesson has several tasks, and each module ends with a big practice lesson.\n- **Deeper topics.** Your own allocators, your own Future, lock-free code, macros, web, databases.\n- **Home assignments.** Some things don't fit into one Playground file: a web server, your own procedural macro, firmware for a board. You do those on your own computer.\n\nThe rules stay the same: short steps, \"Go deeper\" can be skipped, progress saves by itself.\n\n> **Analogy:** before, you built models from instructions. Now you have a workbench, tools and blueprints — and you machine the parts yourself."},{"t":"text","title":"Home assignments","md":"A \"Home assignment\" card looks like this:\n- what to do — step by step, with `cargo` commands;\n- a **self-check** list: items you tick yourself.\n\nThe site doesn't run that code. The ticks are for you: you can see what's done. You can move on without them.\n\nFor home assignments you need Rust on your computer. It installs with one command from **rustup.rs**."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does the site do with a home assignment?","options":["Checks the code automatically","Remembers the self-check items you ticked","Won't let you move on until everything is ticked"],"answer":1,"why":["No, that code runs on your computer.","Right.","Nothing is locked: you can always move on."]},{"t":"homework","title":"Set up your workbench","md":"Install Rust and check that everything works.\n\n1. Install Rust from **rustup.rs** (Windows: download `rustup-init.exe`; macOS and Linux: one terminal command).\n2. Check the versions:\n```sh\nrustc --version\ncargo --version\n```\n3. Create a project and run it:\n```sh\ncargo new workshop\ncd workshop\ncargo run\n```\n4. Install an editor with hints: VS Code + the **rust-analyzer** extension (or RustRover).","check":["`cargo --version` prints a version","`cargo run` in the new project prints `Hello, world!`","The editor shows hints and underlines errors"]},{"t":"recap","items":["The workshop: lots of practice and deep topics.","Home assignments are done on your computer; you tick the self-check by hand.","Rust installs through rustup.rs."]}],"goals":["See how the workshop differs from the first two levels","Learn what home assignments are"]},{"id":"x00-2","title":"Reading compiler errors like a pro","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"An error is a hint","md":"At Level 3 compiler messages get longer: generics, lifetimes, traits. But they're all built the same way:\n1. The **error code** — `error[E0502]`.\n2. The **main sentence** — what's wrong.\n3. The **arrows** under the code — where exactly.\n4. **help** and **note** — how to fix it. Often there's a ready-made fix.\n\nRead top to bottom and look at the **first** error first: the others are often its consequences.\n\n> **Analogy:** a sat-nav says \"in 200 meters, turn right\". It isn't scolding you — it's showing you the way."},{"t":"text","title":"rustc --explain","md":"Every error has a detailed explanation with examples:\n```sh\nrustc --explain E0502\n```\nThe same is on the web: the \"Rust error codes index\". In the Playground you can just click the error code."},{"t":"code","fails":true,"md":"This code won't compile. Run it and read the error: the code, the main sentence, the arrows, the help.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut list = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    let first = &list[0];\n    list.push(4);\n    println!(\"{first}\");\n}","after":"Error `E0502`: you can't change `list` (`push`) while the reference `first` is alive. Fix it by moving the `println!` above the `push`, or by copying the number: `let first = list[0];`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"The compiler output has five errors. Which do you start with?","options":["The first one","The last one","The shortest one"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. The others often follow from it.","The last ones are usually consequences of the first.","Length says nothing about importance."]},{"t":"task","md":"Fix the code without changing its meaning: first print the first element, then add `4` and print the length.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut list = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    let first = &list[0];\n    list.push(4);\n    println!(\"{first}\");\n    println!(\"{}\", list.len());\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut list = vec![1, 2, 3];\n    let first = list[0];\n    list.push(4);\n    println!(\"{first}\");\n    println!(\"{}\", list.len());\n}","expect":"1\n4","hints":["`i32` is `Copy`. Take a copy of the number instead of a reference: `let first = list[0];`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Fix error `E0382` (use after move): the function should only **look** at the string.","code":"fn shout(s: String) -> String {\n    s.to_uppercase()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"ferris\");\n    println!(\"{}\", shout(name));\n    println!(\"{name}\");\n}","solution":"fn shout(s: &str) -> String {\n    s.to_uppercase()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let name = String::from(\"ferris\");\n    println!(\"{}\", shout(&name));\n    println!(\"{name}\");\n}","expect":"FERRIS\nferris","hints":["Let the function take `&str`, and call it as `shout(&name)`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Error code, main sentence, arrows, help — read them in order.","Start with the first error.","`rustc --explain E0XXX` — a detailed explanation."]}],"goals":["Break long compiler messages into parts","Use `rustc --explain`"]},{"id":"x00-3","title":"How workshop tasks work","min":6,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Tasks got bigger","md":"Many tasks are now checked not by output but by **tests**: the code has a `#[cfg(test)] mod tests`, and the site runs `cargo test`. The task is solved when all tests are green.\n\nThat's how real projects are checked: not \"did it print the right thing\", but \"does the function work on different inputs\"."},{"t":"text","title":"How to solve a big task","md":"1. Read the description and the **tests**: they're more precise than any text.\n2. First get the code to **compile** — `todo!()` inside is fine.\n3. Then fix the tests one at a time.\n4. Stuck? Open a hint. That's not shameful, it's a tool.\n\n> **Analogy:** tests are like a blueprint with dimensions. The part is done when every dimension matches."},{"t":"task","tests":true,"md":"Finish `median` so the tests pass. An empty slice gives `None`; with an even length, it's the average of the two middle values (rounded down).","code":"pub fn median(v: &[i32]) -> Option<i32> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn empty() {\n        assert_eq!(median(&[]), None);\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn odd() {\n        assert_eq!(median(&[5, 1, 3]), Some(3));\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn even() {\n        assert_eq!(median(&[4, 1, 3, 2]), Some(2));\n    }\n}","solution":"pub fn median(v: &[i32]) -> Option<i32> {\n    if v.is_empty() {\n        return None;\n    }\n    let mut s = v.to_vec();\n    s.sort();\n    let n = s.len();\n    if n % 2 == 1 {\n        Some(s[n / 2])\n    } else {\n        Some((s[n / 2 - 1] + s[n / 2]) / 2)\n    }\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn empty() {\n        assert_eq!(median(&[]), None);\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn odd() {\n        assert_eq!(median(&[5, 1, 3]), Some(3));\n    }\n\n    #[test]\n    fn even() {\n        assert_eq!(median(&[4, 1, 3, 2]), Some(2));\n    }\n}","hints":["Copy the slice into a `Vec` (`v.to_vec()`) and sort it.","For `[1, 2, 3, 4]` the middle indexes are `1` and `2`: `(2 + 3) / 2 = 2`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Tasks with tests: solved when all tests pass.","Compile first (with `todo!()` if needed), then fix the tests.","Tests are the most precise task description."]}],"goals":["Learn the format of Level 3 tasks","Get used to tests inside tasks"]},{"id":"x00-p","title":"Practice: workshop warm-up","min":15,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"The `Pairs` iterator yields neighboring pairs of a slice: for `[1, 2, 3]` — `(1, 2)`, `(2, 3)`. Finish `next`.","code":"struct Pairs<'a> {\n    items: &'a [i32],\n    pos: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Iterator for Pairs<'a> {\n    type Item = (i32, i32);\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<(i32, i32)> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<(i32, i32)> = Pairs { items: &[1, 2, 3, 4], pos: 0 }.collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"struct Pairs<'a> {\n    items: &'a [i32],\n    pos: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Iterator for Pairs<'a> {\n    type Item = (i32, i32);\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<(i32, i32)> {\n        if self.pos + 1 >= self.items.len() {\n            return None;\n        }\n        let r = (self.items[self.pos], self.items[self.pos + 1]);\n        self.pos += 1;\n        Some(r)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<(i32, i32)> = Pairs { items: &[1, 2, 3, 4], pos: 0 }.collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"[(1, 2), (2, 3), (3, 4)]","hints":["If `pos + 1` is already past the end — `None`."]},{"t":"task","tests":true,"md":"The `group_by_len` function sorts words by length into a `BTreeMap<usize, Vec<&str>>`. Finish it so the tests pass.","code":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\npub fn group_by_len<'a>(words: &[&'a str]) -> BTreeMap<usize, Vec<&'a str>> {\n    todo!()\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn groups() {\n        let g = group_by_len(&[\"cat\", \"ox\", \"dog\", \"rust\"]);\n        assert_eq!(g[&2], vec![\"ox\"]);\n        assert_eq!(g[&3], vec![\"cat\", \"dog\"]);\n        assert_eq!(g[&4], vec![\"rust\"]);\n    }\n}","solution":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\npub fn group_by_len<'a>(words: &[&'a str]) -> BTreeMap<usize, Vec<&'a str>> {\n    let mut g: BTreeMap<usize, Vec<&'a str>> = BTreeMap::new();\n    for w in words {\n        g.entry(w.chars().count()).or_default().push(*w);\n    }\n    g\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn groups() {\n        let g = group_by_len(&[\"cat\", \"ox\", \"dog\", \"rust\"]);\n        assert_eq!(g[&2], vec![\"ox\"]);\n        assert_eq!(g[&3], vec![\"cat\", \"dog\"]);\n        assert_eq!(g[&4], vec![\"rust\"]);\n    }\n}","hints":["Count the length in characters: `w.chars().count()`.","`g.entry(len).or_default().push(*w)` — creates an empty `Vec` the first time."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write `impl From<&str> for Rgb` that parses `\"#ff8000\"` into three bytes. A bad string gives black, `Rgb(0, 0, 0)`.","code":"#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]\nstruct Rgb(u8, u8, u8);\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Rgb = \"#ff8000\".into();\n    let b: Rgb = \"oops\".into();\n    println!(\"{a:?} {b:?}\");\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug, PartialEq)]\nstruct Rgb(u8, u8, u8);\n\nimpl From<&str> for Rgb {\n    fn from(s: &str) -> Self {\n        let parse = |i: usize| s.get(i..i + 2).and_then(|h| u8::from_str_radix(h, 16).ok());\n        if s.len() == 7 && s.starts_with('#') {\n            if let (Some(r), Some(g), Some(b)) = (parse(1), parse(3), parse(5)) {\n                return Rgb(r, g, b);\n            }\n        }\n        Rgb(0, 0, 0)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Rgb = \"#ff8000\".into();\n    let b: Rgb = \"oops\".into();\n    println!(\"{a:?} {b:?}\");\n}","expect":"Rgb(255, 128, 0) Rgb(0, 0, 0)","hints":["`u8::from_str_radix(\"ff\", 16)` turns hexadecimal text into a number.","`s.get(1..3)` safely takes a piece of the string and returns `None` if it isn't there."]},{"t":"task","md":"A counter closure: `make_counter()` returns an `impl FnMut() -> u32` that returns the next number on every call, starting at 1.","code":"fn make_counter() -> impl FnMut() -> u32 {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = make_counter();\n    let mut d = make_counter();\n    println!(\"{} {} {}\", c(), c(), c());\n    println!(\"{}\", d());\n}","solution":"fn make_counter() -> impl FnMut() -> u32 {\n    let mut n = 0;\n    move || {\n        n += 1;\n        n\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = make_counter();\n    let mut d = make_counter();\n    println!(\"{} {} {}\", c(), c(), c());\n    println!(\"{}\", d());\n}","expect":"1 2 3\n1","hints":["`let mut n = 0; move || { n += 1; n }` — each counter gets its own `n`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Iterators, `BTreeMap`, `From`, closures with state — the base for the workshop.","If something was hard, revisit that Level 2 module."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Warm up on Level 2 tasks to check yourself"]}]},{"id":"x01","title":"Types, deeper","sub":"Const generics, PhantomData, blanket impls, Sized","lessons":[{"id":"x01-1","title":"Const generics","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A number as a type parameter","md":"Generics can be over a type `<T>`, but also over a **value**: `<const N: usize>`. Then `N` is part of the type, and `Matrix<2>` and `Matrix<3>` are different types.\n\n```\nstruct Buffer<const N: usize> {\n    data: [u8; N],\n}\n```\nYou've seen this already: the array `[T; N]` is exactly a const generic.\n\n> **Analogy:** egg cartons for 6 and for 10. Built the same way, but you can't mix them up: ten eggs don't fit in the six-pack."},{"t":"code","code":"fn sum<const N: usize>(a: [i32; N]) -> i32 {\n    a.iter().sum()\n}\n\nfn first_last<T: Copy, const N: usize>(a: [T; N]) -> Option<(T, T)> {\n    if N == 0 { None } else { Some((a[0], a[N - 1])) }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", sum([1, 2, 3]));\n    println!(\"{}\", sum([10; 5]));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", first_last(['a', 'b', 'c']));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", first_last::<u8, 0>([]));\n}","out":"6\n50\nSome(('a', 'c'))\nNone","after":"`N` is known **at compile time**. `sum` for an array of 3 and of 5 elements are two different copies of the code."},{"t":"text","title":"Where it helps","md":"- Fixed-size vectors and matrices: `Vec3 = Vector<3>`.\n- Heap-free buffers for embedded systems (`heapless::Vec<T, 16>`).\n- Size checks at compile time: the compiler won't let you add a `Vector<3>` and a `Vector<4>`."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What is `N` in `struct Ring<const N: usize>`?","options":["A field of the struct","A number that becomes part of the type","A variable you can change"],"answer":1,"why":["It's a type parameter; it isn't stored in memory.","Right. `Ring<4>` and `Ring<8>` are different types.","The value is known at compile time and doesn't change."]},{"t":"task","md":"Make `Vector<const N: usize>` with an `[f64; N]` field and a `dot` method that computes the dot product with another vector of **the same** size.","code":"struct Vector<const N: usize>([f64; N]);\n\nimpl<const N: usize> Vector<N> {\n    fn dot(&self, other: &Vector<N>) -> f64 {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Vector([1.0, 2.0, 3.0]);\n    let b = Vector([4.0, 5.0, 6.0]);\n    println!(\"{}\", a.dot(&b));\n}","solution":"struct Vector<const N: usize>([f64; N]);\n\nimpl<const N: usize> Vector<N> {\n    fn dot(&self, other: &Vector<N>) -> f64 {\n        self.0.iter().zip(other.0.iter()).map(|(x, y)| x * y).sum()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = Vector([1.0, 2.0, 3.0]);\n    let b = Vector([4.0, 5.0, 6.0]);\n    println!(\"{}\", a.dot(&b));\n}","expect":"32","hints":["`self.0.iter().zip(other.0.iter()).map(|(x, y)| x * y).sum()`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The ring buffer `Ring<const N: usize>` keeps the last `N` numbers. Finish `push` (overwrites the oldest) and `items` (returns them from oldest to newest).","code":"struct Ring<const N: usize> {\n    buf: [i32; N],\n    start: usize,\n    len: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<const N: usize> Ring<N> {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        Ring { buf: [0; N], start: 0, len: 0 }\n    }\n    fn push(&mut self, x: i32) {\n        // your code\n    }\n    fn items(&self) -> Vec<i32> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut r: Ring<3> = Ring::new();\n    for x in 1..=5 {\n        r.push(x);\n    }\n    println!(\"{:?}\", r.items());\n}","solution":"struct Ring<const N: usize> {\n    buf: [i32; N],\n    start: usize,\n    len: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<const N: usize> Ring<N> {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        Ring { buf: [0; N], start: 0, len: 0 }\n    }\n    fn push(&mut self, x: i32) {\n        if self.len < N {\n            self.buf[(self.start + self.len) % N] = x;\n            self.len += 1;\n        } else {\n            self.buf[self.start] = x;\n            self.start = (self.start + 1) % N;\n        }\n    }\n    fn items(&self) -> Vec<i32> {\n        (0..self.len).map(|i| self.buf[(self.start + i) % N]).collect()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut r: Ring<3> = Ring::new();\n    for x in 1..=5 {\n        r.push(x);\n    }\n    println!(\"{:?}\", r.items());\n}","expect":"[3, 4, 5]","hints":["While the buffer isn't full, write at `(start + len) % N` and increase `len`.","When it's full, write at `start` (the oldest) and move `start` by one."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`<const N: usize>` — a generic over a number. `N` is part of the type.","Functions for arrays of any length: `fn f<const N: usize>(a: [T; N])`.","Sizes are checked at compile time."]}],"goals":["Make generics over numbers, not just types","Write functions for arrays of any length"]},{"id":"x01-2","title":"PhantomData and marker types","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Same data, different meaning","md":"`Distance<Meters>` and `Distance<Feet>` both hold the same `f64` inside. But you can't add them together.\n\nThe `Unit` parameter isn't used in the data. The compiler doesn't like that: \"parameter is never used\". That's what `PhantomData<Unit>` is for — a zero-size field that says \"this type is sort of here\".\n\n> **Analogy:** colored stickers on identical jars. Same jar, but red is for spices and blue is for grains."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::marker::PhantomData;\nuse std::ops::Add;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Meters;\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Feet;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy)]\nstruct Distance<U> {\n    value: f64,\n    unit: PhantomData<U>,\n}\n\nimpl<U> Distance<U> {\n    fn new(value: f64) -> Self {\n        Distance { value, unit: PhantomData }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<U> Add for Distance<U> {\n    type Output = Distance<U>;\n    fn add(self, o: Self) -> Self {\n        Distance::new(self.value + o.value)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Distance<Meters> = Distance::new(5.0);\n    let b: Distance<Meters> = Distance::new(2.5);\n    println!(\"{}\", (a + b).value);\n    println!(\"{}\", std::mem::size_of::<Distance<Feet>>());\n    // let c: Distance<Feet> = Distance::new(1.0);\n    // a + c; // error: different units\n}","out":"7.5\n8","after":"`Distance<Feet>` is 8 bytes, like a single `f64`. The marker weighs nothing."},{"t":"quiz","md":"How much memory does a `PhantomData<T>` field take?","options":["Zero bytes","As much as `T`","One pointer"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. It's only information for the compiler.","No, `T` isn't stored there.","There's no pointer at all."]},{"t":"task","md":"Add a `to_feet` method to `Distance<Meters>` that returns a `Distance<Feet>` (1 m = 3.28084 ft). The method must exist **only** on meters.","code":"use std::marker::PhantomData;\n\nstruct Meters;\nstruct Feet;\n\nstruct Distance<U> {\n    value: f64,\n    unit: PhantomData<U>,\n}\n\nimpl<U> Distance<U> {\n    fn new(value: f64) -> Self {\n        Distance { value, unit: PhantomData }\n    }\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let m: Distance<Meters> = Distance::new(10.0);\n    let f: Distance<Feet> = m.to_feet();\n    println!(\"{:.1}\", f.value);\n}","solution":"use std::marker::PhantomData;\n\nstruct Meters;\nstruct Feet;\n\nstruct Distance<U> {\n    value: f64,\n    unit: PhantomData<U>,\n}\n\nimpl<U> Distance<U> {\n    fn new(value: f64) -> Self {\n        Distance { value, unit: PhantomData }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Distance<Meters> {\n    fn to_feet(&self) -> Distance<Feet> {\n        Distance::new(self.value * 3.28084)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let m: Distance<Meters> = Distance::new(10.0);\n    let f: Distance<Feet> = m.to_feet();\n    println!(\"{:.1}\", f.value);\n}","expect":"32.8","hints":["A separate `impl Distance<Meters> { ... }` block — methods only for meters."]},{"t":"task","md":"Typed identifiers: `Id<User>` and `Id<Order>` both hold a `u64`, but can't be mixed up. Finish `Id::new` and the `find_user` function, which accepts only `Id<User>`.","code":"use std::marker::PhantomData;\n\nstruct User;\nstruct Order;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nstruct Id<T> {\n    raw: u64,\n    kind: PhantomData<T>,\n}\n\nimpl<T> Id<T> {\n    // your code: fn new(raw: u64) -> Self\n}\n\nfn find_user(id: Id<User>) -> &'static str {\n    // return \"Ada\" if raw == 1, otherwise \"nobody\"\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let u: Id<User> = Id::new(1);\n    let _o: Id<Order> = Id::new(1);\n    println!(\"{}\", find_user(u));\n    println!(\"{}\", find_user(Id::new(7)));\n}","solution":"use std::marker::PhantomData;\n\nstruct User;\nstruct Order;\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nstruct Id<T> {\n    raw: u64,\n    kind: PhantomData<T>,\n}\n\nimpl<T> Id<T> {\n    fn new(raw: u64) -> Self {\n        Id { raw, kind: PhantomData }\n    }\n}\n\nfn find_user(id: Id<User>) -> &'static str {\n    if id.raw == 1 { \"Ada\" } else { \"nobody\" }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let u: Id<User> = Id::new(1);\n    let _o: Id<Order> = Id::new(1);\n    println!(\"{}\", find_user(u));\n    println!(\"{}\", find_user(Id::new(7)));\n}","expect":"Ada\nnobody","hints":["`fn new(raw: u64) -> Self { Id { raw, kind: PhantomData } }`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"derive and PhantomData: a catch","md":"`#[derive(Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]` on `Id<T>` requires that **`T` too** is `Clone`/`Copy`/`PartialEq`. Even though `T` isn't stored inside! That's how `derive` works: it adds a bound on every parameter.\n\nSo the `Id<User>` from the task won't be `Copy` unless `User` is `Copy`. The workaround is a manual implementation:\n\n```\nimpl<T> Clone for Id<T> { fn clone(&self) -> Self { *self } }\nimpl<T> Copy for Id<T> {}\n```\nOr crates like `derive-where`, where you can state the bounds by hand."},{"t":"recap","items":["`PhantomData<T>` is a zero-size type marker.","Same data + different markers = different types.","`impl Type<Marker>` — methods only for a specific marker."]}],"goals":["Tag a type with information that isn't in the data","Make units of measure that can't be mixed up"]},{"id":"x01-3","title":"Blanket impls and extension traits","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"An implementation \"for everyone\"","md":"A **blanket impl** implements a trait for **any** type that meets a condition:\n\n```\nimpl<T: Display> MyTrait for T { ... }\n```\nThe standard library is built this way: `impl<T: Display> ToString for T` — that's why everything that can `Display` has `.to_string()`."},{"t":"text","title":"Extension trait","md":"You can't add a method directly to `str` or `Vec` — they aren't yours. But you can declare **your own** trait with a method and implement it for someone else's type. This is called an **extension trait**:\n\n```\ntrait Shout {\n    fn shout(&self) -> String;\n}\nimpl Shout for str {\n    fn shout(&self) -> String { self.to_uppercase() + \"!\" }\n}\n// \"hello\".shout() == \"HELLO!\"\n```\nThe orphan rule isn't broken: the trait is yours.\n\n> **Analogy:** a drill attachment. The drill isn't yours, but the attachment is — and the drill gets a new function."},{"t":"code","md":"An extension for **every iterator** of numbers — a blanket impl and an extension trait together:","code":"trait Stats: Iterator<Item = i32> + Sized {\n    fn mean(self) -> Option<f64> {\n        let (sum, n) = self.fold((0i64, 0u32), |(s, n), x| (s + x as i64, n + 1));\n        if n == 0 { None } else { Some(sum as f64 / n as f64) }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<I: Iterator<Item = i32>> Stats for I {}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", vec![1, 2, 3, 4].into_iter().mean());\n    println!(\"{:?}\", (1..=10).filter(|x| x % 2 == 0).mean());\n    println!(\"{:?}\", std::iter::empty::<i32>().mean());\n}","out":"Some(2.5)\nSome(6.0)\nNone","after":"Now **any** `i32` iterator has `.mean()` — even ones that don't exist yet."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why does every type with `Display` have a `.to_string()` method?","options":["Someone wrote it by hand for each type","The standard library has a blanket impl of `ToString` for all `T: Display`","The compiler adds it by itself"],"answer":1,"why":["That would be impossible.","Right.","It's ordinary library code."]},{"t":"task","md":"Make an extension trait `CountWords` with a method `word_count(&self) -> usize` and implement it for `str`.","code":"// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", \"  the cat   sat \".word_count());\n    let s = String::from(\"one two\");\n    println!(\"{}\", s.word_count());\n}","solution":"trait CountWords {\n    fn word_count(&self) -> usize;\n}\n\nimpl CountWords for str {\n    fn word_count(&self) -> usize {\n        self.split_whitespace().count()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", \"  the cat   sat \".word_count());\n    let s = String::from(\"one two\");\n    println!(\"{}\", s.word_count());\n}","expect":"3\n2","hints":["`impl CountWords for str { ... }` — and `String` gets the method too, through `Deref` to `str`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A blanket impl: the `Describe` trait has a method `describe(&self) -> String` that returns `<value> (N chars)`. Implement it for all `T: Display` at once.","code":"use std::fmt::Display;\n\ntrait Describe {\n    fn describe(&self) -> String;\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", 42.describe());\n    println!(\"{}\", \"crab\".describe());\n}","solution":"use std::fmt::Display;\n\ntrait Describe {\n    fn describe(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nimpl<T: Display> Describe for T {\n    fn describe(&self) -> String {\n        let s = self.to_string();\n        format!(\"{} ({} chars)\", s, s.chars().count())\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", 42.describe());\n    println!(\"{}\", \"crab\".describe());\n}","expect":"42 (2 chars)\ncrab (4 chars)","hints":["`impl<T: Display> Describe for T { ... }`.","Inside: `let s = self.to_string();` and `s.chars().count()`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Blanket impl: `impl<T: Condition> Trait for T`.","An extension trait adds methods to other people's types.","Together they give methods to whole families of types."]}],"goals":["Implement a trait for all fitting types at once","Add your own methods to other people's types"]},{"id":"x01-4","title":"Sized and ?Sized","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Not every type has a known size","md":"Most types have a size known at compile time: `i32` is 4 bytes, `String` is 24 bytes. That's the `Sized` trait.\n\nBut some types have **no** known size:\n- `str` — a string of any length;\n- `[T]` — a slice of any length;\n- `dyn Trait` — some type with a trait.\n\nYou can't put them in a variable directly — only behind a pointer: `&str`, `Box<[T]>`, `&dyn Trait`."},{"t":"text","title":"Implicit Sized","md":"Every `<T>` in a generic really means `<T: Sized>`. The compiler adds it by itself. To allow unsized types as well, you write `?Sized` — \"maybe not Sized\":\n\n```\nfn show<T: Display + ?Sized>(x: &T) { println!(\"{x}\"); }\n```\nNow `show` accepts `&i32`, `&str` and `&dyn Display`.\n\n> **Analogy:** a fixed-size suitcase (`Sized`) versus luggage \"of any size, but only on a trolley\" (`?Sized` behind a pointer)."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::fmt::Display;\n\nfn show<T: Display + ?Sized>(label: &str, x: &T) {\n    println!(\"{label}: {x} ({} bytes behind the reference)\", std::mem::size_of_val(x));\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    show(\"number\", &7u8);\n    show(\"string\", \"hello\");\n    let d: &dyn Display = &3.5f64;\n    show(\"dyn\", d);\n}","out":"number: 7 (1 bytes behind the reference)\nstring: hello (5 bytes behind the reference)\ndyn: 3.5 (8 bytes behind the reference)","after":"`size_of_val` finds the size at run time: the string \"hello\" is 5 bytes."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `T: ?Sized` mean?","options":["`T` definitely has no size","`T` may be unsized, so it's used only behind a pointer","`T` is always on the heap"],"answer":1,"why":["Not \"definitely without\", but \"may be without\".","Right.","Not necessarily: a `&str` can point anywhere."]},{"t":"task","md":"`longest_of` must accept a slice of **anything** that can turn into `&str` (`String`, `&str`) and return the longest length in characters. `print_all` must also accept a `str`. Add the missing bound.","code":"fn longest_of<T: AsRef<str>>(items: &[T]) -> usize {\n    items.iter().map(|s| s.as_ref().chars().count()).max().unwrap_or(0)\n}\n\nfn print_all<T: std::fmt::Display>(items: &T) {\n    println!(\"{items}\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", longest_of(&[\"cat\", \"crocodile\"]));\n    println!(\"{}\", longest_of(&[String::from(\"ab\")]));\n    print_all(\"an unsized string\");\n}","solution":"fn longest_of<T: AsRef<str>>(items: &[T]) -> usize {\n    items.iter().map(|s| s.as_ref().chars().count()).max().unwrap_or(0)\n}\n\nfn print_all<T: std::fmt::Display + ?Sized>(items: &T) {\n    println!(\"{items}\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", longest_of(&[\"cat\", \"crocodile\"]));\n    println!(\"{}\", longest_of(&[String::from(\"ab\")]));\n    print_all(\"an unsized string\");\n}","expect":"9\n2\nan unsized string","hints":["`print_all(\"...\")` passes a `&str`, so `T = str` — unsized. You need `?Sized`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`Sized` — the size is known at compile time. Every `<T>` has it by default.","`str`, `[T]`, `dyn Trait` are unsized and live behind a pointer.","`T: ?Sized` + `&T` — accept such types too."]}],"goals":["Understand what a type without a known size is","Write functions that accept `str`, `[T]` and `dyn Trait`"]},{"id":"x01-p","title":"Practice: types","min":20,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"A matrix `Matrix<const R: usize, const C: usize>` and its transpose: an `R×C` matrix becomes `C×R`. Finish `transpose`.","code":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Matrix<const R: usize, const C: usize>([[i32; C]; R]);\n\nimpl<const R: usize, const C: usize> Matrix<R, C> {\n    fn transpose(&self) -> Matrix<C, R> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let m = Matrix([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", m.transpose());\n}","solution":"#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Matrix<const R: usize, const C: usize>([[i32; C]; R]);\n\nimpl<const R: usize, const C: usize> Matrix<R, C> {\n    fn transpose(&self) -> Matrix<C, R> {\n        let mut out = [[0; R]; C];\n        for r in 0..R {\n            for c in 0..C {\n                out[c][r] = self.0[r][c];\n            }\n        }\n        Matrix(out)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let m = Matrix([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", m.transpose());\n}","expect":"Matrix([[1, 4], [2, 5], [3, 6]])","hints":["Create `[[0; R]; C]` and copy `out[c][r] = self.0[r][c]`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Money in different currencies: `Money<Usd>` and `Money<Eur>` can't be added together. Implement `Add` only for the same currency, and `Display`, which prints the amount with the currency code from the associated constant `CODE`.","code":"use std::fmt;\nuse std::marker::PhantomData;\nuse std::ops::Add;\n\ntrait Currency {\n    const CODE: &'static str;\n}\nstruct Usd;\nstruct Eur;\nimpl Currency for Usd { const CODE: &'static str = \"USD\"; }\nimpl Currency for Eur { const CODE: &'static str = \"EUR\"; }\n\nstruct Money<C> {\n    cents: i64,\n    cur: PhantomData<C>,\n}\n\nimpl<C> Money<C> {\n    fn new(cents: i64) -> Self {\n        Money { cents, cur: PhantomData }\n    }\n}\n\n// your code: Add and Display\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Money<Usd> = Money::new(1050);\n    let b: Money<Usd> = Money::new(275);\n    println!(\"{}\", a + b);\n    println!(\"{}\", Money::<Eur>::new(99));\n}","solution":"use std::fmt;\nuse std::marker::PhantomData;\nuse std::ops::Add;\n\ntrait Currency {\n    const CODE: &'static str;\n}\nstruct Usd;\nstruct Eur;\nimpl Currency for Usd { const CODE: &'static str = \"USD\"; }\nimpl Currency for Eur { const CODE: &'static str = \"EUR\"; }\n\nstruct Money<C> {\n    cents: i64,\n    cur: PhantomData<C>,\n}\n\nimpl<C> Money<C> {\n    fn new(cents: i64) -> Self {\n        Money { cents, cur: PhantomData }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<C> Add for Money<C> {\n    type Output = Money<C>;\n    fn add(self, o: Self) -> Self {\n        Money::new(self.cents + o.cents)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<C: Currency> fmt::Display for Money<C> {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut fmt::Formatter) -> fmt::Result {\n        write!(f, \"{}.{:02} {}\", self.cents / 100, self.cents % 100, C::CODE)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a: Money<Usd> = Money::new(1050);\n    let b: Money<Usd> = Money::new(275);\n    println!(\"{}\", a + b);\n    println!(\"{}\", Money::<Eur>::new(99));\n}","expect":"13.25 USD\n0.99 EUR","hints":["`impl<C> Add for Money<C>` — only matching `C`s add up.","`Display` needs `C: Currency` to read `C::CODE`."]},{"t":"task","md":"An extension trait `ChunkSum` for **all slices** of numbers: the method `chunk_sums(n)` returns the sums of chunks of `n` items. Write a blanket implementation for `[T]` where `T: Copy + std::iter::Sum`.","code":"trait ChunkSum<T> {\n    fn chunk_sums(&self, n: usize) -> Vec<T>;\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].chunk_sums(2));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", vec![0.5, 1.5, 2.0].chunk_sums(3));\n}","solution":"trait ChunkSum<T> {\n    fn chunk_sums(&self, n: usize) -> Vec<T>;\n}\n\nimpl<T: Copy + std::iter::Sum> ChunkSum<T> for [T] {\n    fn chunk_sums(&self, n: usize) -> Vec<T> {\n        self.chunks(n).map(|c| c.iter().copied().sum()).collect()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", [1, 2, 3, 4, 5].chunk_sums(2));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", vec![0.5, 1.5, 2.0].chunk_sums(3));\n}","expect":"[3, 7, 5]\n[4.0]","hints":["`impl<T: Copy + std::iter::Sum> ChunkSum<T> for [T]`.","`self.chunks(n).map(|c| c.iter().copied().sum()).collect()`."]},{"t":"task","md":"`print_ref` prints any value, including unsized ones and `dyn Display`. Fix its signature so every call compiles.","code":"use std::fmt::Display;\n\nfn print_ref<T: Display>(x: &T) {\n    println!(\"> {x}\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    print_ref(&5);\n    print_ref(\"str\");\n    let items: Vec<Box<dyn Display>> = vec![Box::new(1.5), Box::new(\"two\")];\n    for it in &items {\n        print_ref(it.as_ref());\n    }\n}","solution":"use std::fmt::Display;\n\nfn print_ref<T: Display + ?Sized>(x: &T) {\n    println!(\"> {x}\");\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    print_ref(&5);\n    print_ref(\"str\");\n    let items: Vec<Box<dyn Display>> = vec![Box::new(1.5), Box::new(\"two\")];\n    for it in &items {\n        print_ref(it.as_ref());\n    }\n}","expect":"> 5\n> str\n> 1.5\n> two","hints":["Both `str` and `dyn Display` are unsized. Add `?Sized`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Constants in types — sizes checked at compile time.","`PhantomData` + associated constants — markers that carry data for printing.","A blanket impl for `[T]` — methods for every slice.","`?Sized` — accept `str` and `dyn Trait`."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Use const generics, PhantomData, blanket impls and ?Sized"]}]},{"id":"x02","title":"Fine points of traits","sub":"dyn compatibility, upcasting, Any, GATs, impl Trait and async fn in traits","lessons":[{"id":"x02-1","title":"What dyn can and can't do","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The dyn compatibility rules","md":"To use a trait as `dyn Trait`, the compiler must build a **method table**. That's impossible if a method:\n- returns `Self` (the size of `Self` is unknown behind `dyn`);\n- is generic over its own parameter `<T>` (that would need an endless table — one entry per `T`);\n- doesn't take `self` at all (a static method like `fn new()`).\n\nSuch a trait is called **not dyn compatible**.\n\n> **Analogy:** a TV remote: its buttons are known in advance. You can't put a \"do that thing, whatever it is\" button on a remote."},{"t":"text","title":"Switch a method off for dyn","md":"If a trait has one \"awkward\" method, you can **exclude** it from the table:\n\n```\ntrait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64;\n    fn scaled(&self, k: f64) -> Self where Self: Sized; // not for dyn\n}\n```\n`where Self: Sized` means \"this method is only for concrete types\". The other methods still work through `dyn Shape`."},{"t":"code","code":"trait Shape {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64;\n    fn name(&self) -> String {\n        String::from(\"shape\")\n    }\n    fn scaled(&self, k: f64) -> Self\n    where\n        Self: Sized;\n}\n\n#[derive(Debug)]\nstruct Square(f64);\n\nimpl Shape for Square {\n    fn area(&self) -> f64 { self.0 * self.0 }\n    fn scaled(&self, k: f64) -> Self { Square(self.0 * k) }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let big = Square(2.0).scaled(3.0);\n    println!(\"{big:?}\");\n    let shapes: Vec<Box<dyn Shape>> = vec![Box::new(big), Box::new(Square(1.0))];\n    for s in &shapes {\n        println!(\"{}: {}\", s.name(), s.area());\n    }\n}","out":"Square(6.0)\nshape: 36\nshape: 1","after":"Remove `where Self: Sized` and look at the error: the trait stops being dyn compatible."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which method makes a trait not dyn compatible?","options":["`fn area(&self) -> f64`","`fn clone_me(&self) -> Self`","`fn name(&self) -> String`"],"answer":1,"why":["An ordinary method, it's fine.","Right. The size of `Self` is unknown behind `dyn`.","`String` is a known type, it's fine."]},{"t":"task","md":"The `Animal` trait doesn't compile as `dyn` because it has `fn new(name: &str) -> Self`. Exclude that method from the table so `Vec<Box<dyn Animal>>` works.","code":"trait Animal {\n    fn new(name: &str) -> Self;\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Cat(String);\n\nimpl Animal for Cat {\n    fn new(name: &str) -> Self { Cat(name.to_string()) }\n    fn speak(&self) -> String { format!(\"{}: meow\", self.0) }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let zoo: Vec<Box<dyn Animal>> = vec![Box::new(Cat::new(\"Kitty\"))];\n    for a in &zoo {\n        println!(\"{}\", a.speak());\n    }\n}","solution":"trait Animal {\n    fn new(name: &str) -> Self\n    where\n        Self: Sized;\n    fn speak(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Cat(String);\n\nimpl Animal for Cat {\n    fn new(name: &str) -> Self { Cat(name.to_string()) }\n    fn speak(&self) -> String { format!(\"{}: meow\", self.0) }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let zoo: Vec<Box<dyn Animal>> = vec![Box::new(Cat::new(\"Kitty\"))];\n    for a in &zoo {\n        println!(\"{}\", a.speak());\n    }\n}","expect":"Kitty: meow","hints":["Add the condition `where Self: Sized` to `new`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`dyn` needs a trait without `-> Self`, without generic methods and without methods lacking `self`.","`where Self: Sized` removes a method from the `dyn` table.","The other methods still work through `dyn`."]}],"goals":["See why not every trait can be used as `dyn`","Exclude single methods with `where Self: Sized`"]},{"id":"x02-2","title":"Upcasting and Any","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Upcasting","md":"If `trait Pet: Animal` (Pet requires Animal), any `&dyn Pet` can be turned into a `&dyn Animal`. That's **upcasting** — moving to a more general trait. You write it as a plain assignment.\n\n```\nlet p: &dyn Pet = &dog;\nlet a: &dyn Animal = p; // upcast\n```"},{"t":"code","code":"trait Animal {\n    fn name(&self) -> String;\n}\ntrait Pet: Animal {\n    fn owner(&self) -> String;\n}\n\nstruct Dog;\nimpl Animal for Dog {\n    fn name(&self) -> String { String::from(\"Rex\") }\n}\nimpl Pet for Dog {\n    fn owner(&self) -> String { String::from(\"Uncle Theo\") }\n}\n\nfn describe(a: &dyn Animal) {\n    println!(\"animal: {}\", a.name());\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let pets: Vec<Box<dyn Pet>> = vec![Box::new(Dog)];\n    for p in &pets {\n        println!(\"owner: {}\", p.owner());\n        describe(p.as_ref()); // &dyn Pet -> &dyn Animal\n    }\n}","out":"owner: Uncle Theo\nanimal: Rex"},{"t":"text","title":"Any — find out who's in there","md":"`std::any::Any` is a trait almost every type has. Through a `dyn Any` you can ask \"are you a `String`?\" — and get a reference to the concrete type.\n\n```\nuse std::any::Any;\nfn inspect(x: &dyn Any) {\n    if let Some(s) = x.downcast_ref::<String>() { println!(\"string {s}\"); }\n}\n```\nThis is **downcasting** — moving to a concrete type. It's used rarely: an `enum` is usually better. But for plugins and mixed storage it's a lifesaver."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::any::Any;\n\nfn inspect(x: &dyn Any) -> String {\n    if let Some(n) = x.downcast_ref::<i32>() {\n        format!(\"number {n}\")\n    } else if let Some(s) = x.downcast_ref::<String>() {\n        format!(\"string \\\"{s}\\\"\")\n    } else {\n        String::from(\"something else\")\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let items: Vec<Box<dyn Any>> = vec![Box::new(7), Box::new(String::from(\"cat\")), Box::new(1.5)];\n    for it in &items {\n        println!(\"{}\", inspect(it.as_ref()));\n    }\n}","out":"number 7\nstring \"cat\"\nsomething else"},{"t":"quiz","md":"What is downcasting?","options":["Turning a `&dyn Pet` into a `&dyn Animal`","Trying to get a concrete type out of a `dyn Any`","Casting a number to a smaller type"],"answer":1,"why":["That's upcasting: to something more general.","Right. It may fail — hence the `Option`.","That's `as`, a different thing."]},{"t":"task","md":"A component store: `Vec<Box<dyn Any>>`. Write `sum_ints`, which adds up only the values of type `i32` and skips the rest.","code":"use std::any::Any;\n\nfn sum_ints(items: &[Box<dyn Any>]) -> i32 {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let items: Vec<Box<dyn Any>> = vec![\n        Box::new(10),\n        Box::new(\"text\"),\n        Box::new(5),\n        Box::new(2.5f64),\n    ];\n    println!(\"{}\", sum_ints(&items));\n}","solution":"use std::any::Any;\n\nfn sum_ints(items: &[Box<dyn Any>]) -> i32 {\n    items.iter().filter_map(|b| b.downcast_ref::<i32>()).sum()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let items: Vec<Box<dyn Any>> = vec![\n        Box::new(10),\n        Box::new(\"text\"),\n        Box::new(5),\n        Box::new(2.5f64),\n    ];\n    println!(\"{}\", sum_ints(&items));\n}","expect":"15","hints":["`b.downcast_ref::<i32>()` returns an `Option<&i32>`. `filter_map` fits."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Upcasting: `&dyn Sub` → `&dyn Super` when `trait Sub: Super`.","`dyn Any` + `downcast_ref::<T>()` — find out the concrete type.","Downcasting is a last resort; an `enum` is usually better."]}],"goals":["Turn a `&dyn Sub` into a `&dyn Super`","Find out the concrete type behind a `dyn Any`"]},{"id":"x02-3","title":"GATs: a lending iterator","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The problem: an iterator can't lend itself","md":"A normal `Iterator` yields items that live **independently** of the `next` call. So you can't write an iterator that returns a `&mut` into **its own** buffer on every step: two such items at once would be two `&mut` to the same thing.\n\nYou need a trait where the item type **depends on how long the iterator itself is borrowed**:\n\n```\ntrait LendingIterator {\n    type Item<'a> where Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item<'_>>;\n}\n```\n`type Item<'a>` is a **generic associated type** (GAT).\n\n> **Analogy:** a librarian lets you read a book in the reading room. You get the next one only after you return this one."},{"t":"code","code":"trait LendingIterator {\n    type Item<'a>\n    where\n        Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item<'_>>;\n}\n\n// Windows of 2 items that you can CHANGE\nstruct WindowsMut<'s> {\n    data: &'s mut [i32],\n    pos: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<'s> LendingIterator for WindowsMut<'s> {\n    type Item<'a> = &'a mut [i32] where Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&mut [i32]> {\n        let start = self.pos;\n        if start + 2 > self.data.len() {\n            return None;\n        }\n        self.pos += 1;\n        Some(&mut self.data[start..start + 2])\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut v = [1, 2, 3, 4];\n    let mut it = WindowsMut { data: &mut v, pos: 0 };\n    while let Some(w) = it.next() {\n        w[1] += w[0]; // running total\n    }\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","out":"[1, 3, 6, 10]","after":"The regular `windows` gives only `&[T]`. Changing through windows became possible thanks to GATs."},{"t":"text","title":"The while let limitation","md":"`for`, `map` and `collect` don't work with a `LendingIterator` — they're built for the normal `Iterator`. You write `while let Some(x) = it.next()`. In exchange it's safe: you can't get the next item while the previous one is alive."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why can't a normal `Iterator` yield `&mut` into its own buffer?","options":["`collect` could gather several `&mut` to the same data at once","You can't return `&mut` from methods at all","The buffer must be on the heap"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. A normal iterator's items live independently of each other.","You can, just not this way.","The heap has nothing to do with it."]},{"t":"task","md":"Finish the `LendingIterator` for `Upper`: each call puts the next line of text, uppercased, into the **internal** `String` buffer and returns a `&str` to that buffer.","code":"trait LendingIterator {\n    type Item<'a>\n    where\n        Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item<'_>>;\n}\n\nstruct Upper<'t> {\n    lines: std::str::Lines<'t>,\n    buf: String,\n}\n\nimpl<'t> LendingIterator for Upper<'t> {\n    type Item<'a> = &'a str where Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&str> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"one\\ntwo\\nthree\";\n    let mut it = Upper { lines: text.lines(), buf: String::new() };\n    while let Some(line) = it.next() {\n        println!(\"{line}\");\n    }\n}","solution":"trait LendingIterator {\n    type Item<'a>\n    where\n        Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item<'_>>;\n}\n\nstruct Upper<'t> {\n    lines: std::str::Lines<'t>,\n    buf: String,\n}\n\nimpl<'t> LendingIterator for Upper<'t> {\n    type Item<'a> = &'a str where Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&str> {\n        let line = self.lines.next()?;\n        self.buf.clear();\n        for c in line.chars() {\n            self.buf.extend(c.to_uppercase());\n        }\n        Some(&self.buf)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"one\\ntwo\\nthree\";\n    let mut it = Upper { lines: text.lines(), buf: String::new() };\n    while let Some(line) = it.next() {\n        println!(\"{line}\");\n    }\n}","expect":"ONE\nTWO\nTHREE","hints":["Take a line: `let line = self.lines.next()?;`. Then `self.buf.clear()` and fill the buffer.","Return a reference to the buffer: `Some(&self.buf)`. One buffer for every step — no copies."]},{"t":"deep","title":"GATs go beyond iterators","md":"GATs are needed wherever an associated type must depend on a parameter: a lifetime or a type.\n\n- **Collection \"families\"**: `type Pointer<T>` — a trait that says \"my smart pointer is `Rc<T>`\" or \"`Arc<T>`\", and code is written for both.\n- **Async traits**, before `async fn` in traits existed, used `type Fut<'a>: Future + 'a`.\n- **Zero-copy parsers** that return pieces of their own buffer.\n\nThe required `where Self: 'a` says: \"a reference to the item won't outlive the iterator itself\". The compiler wants it written explicitly."},{"t":"recap","items":["GAT: `type Item<'a> where Self: 'a;` — the item type depends on the borrow.","A `LendingIterator` yields references into its buffer, one at a time.","Use it with `while let`, not `for`."]}],"goals":["Understand an associated type with a parameter","Write an iterator that yields references into its own buffer"]},{"id":"x02-4","title":"impl Trait and async fn in traits","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Returning impl Trait from a trait method","md":"A trait can have a method that returns \"some iterator\" — each implementation returns its own:\n\n```\ntrait Container {\n    fn items(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = i32>;\n}\n```\nThis used to require `Box<dyn Iterator>` or an associated type. Now it's just `impl`. It's called **RPITIT** (return-position impl Trait in traits)."},{"t":"code","code":"trait Container {\n    fn items(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = i32>;\n    fn total(&self) -> i32 {\n        self.items().sum()\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Evens(i32);\nstruct List(Vec<i32>);\n\nimpl Container for Evens {\n    fn items(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = i32> {\n        (0..=self.0).filter(|x| x % 2 == 0)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Container for List {\n    fn items(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = i32> {\n        self.0.iter().copied()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Evens(6).total());\n    println!(\"{}\", List(vec![5, 5, 1]).total());\n}","out":"12\n11","after":"`Evens` returns a `Filter<...>`, `List` returns a `Copied<...>`. Different types, one trait."},{"t":"text","title":"async fn in a trait","md":"In the same way, you can write `async fn` right in a trait:\n\n```\ntrait Storage {\n    async fn load(&self, key: &str) -> Option<String>;\n}\n```\nUnder the hood it's the same trick: the method returns an `impl Future<Output = ...>`.\n\nOne caveat: such a trait is **not dyn compatible** (`impl` is a concrete but hidden type). For `dyn`, use the `async-trait` crate or a hand-written `Pin<Box<dyn Future>>`."},{"t":"code","md":"Let's check it with the home-made `block_on` from Level 2:","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::pin;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\ntrait Storage {\n    async fn load(&self, key: &str) -> Option<String>;\n}\n\nstruct Memory;\n\nimpl Storage for Memory {\n    async fn load(&self, key: &str) -> Option<String> {\n        match key {\n            \"name\" => Some(String::from(\"Ferris\")),\n            _ => None,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn greet<S: Storage>(s: &S) -> String {\n    let name = s.load(\"name\").await.unwrap_or_default();\n    format!(\"Hello, {name}!\")\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", block_on(greet(&Memory)));\n}","out":"Hello, Ferris!"},{"t":"quiz","md":"Can types with `async fn` in a trait go into a `Vec<Box<dyn Storage>>`?","options":["Yes, no problem","No, `dyn` needs `async-trait` or a hand-written `Pin<Box<dyn Future>>`","Only for `Box`, not for `&`"],"answer":1,"why":["`async fn` in a trait returns a hidden `impl Future`; that's not dyn compatible.","Right.","The kind of pointer doesn't matter."]},{"t":"task","md":"Add a method `long_words(&self, min: usize) -> impl Iterator<Item = &str>` to the `Words` trait that returns words longer than `min` characters. Implement it for `Sentence`.","code":"trait Words {\n    fn long_words(&self, min: usize) -> impl Iterator<Item = &str>;\n}\n\nstruct Sentence(String);\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    let s = Sentence(String::from(\"the cat ate tremendous pies\"));\n    let v: Vec<&str> = s.long_words(4).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"trait Words {\n    fn long_words(&self, min: usize) -> impl Iterator<Item = &str>;\n}\n\nstruct Sentence(String);\n\nimpl Words for Sentence {\n    fn long_words(&self, min: usize) -> impl Iterator<Item = &str> {\n        self.0.split_whitespace().filter(move |w| w.chars().count() > min)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let s = Sentence(String::from(\"the cat ate tremendous pies\"));\n    let v: Vec<&str> = s.long_words(4).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"[\"tremendous\"]","hints":["`self.0.split_whitespace().filter(move |w| w.chars().count() > min)`.","`move` is needed so the closure takes `min` with it."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`fn x(&self) -> impl Trait` right in a trait — each implementation has its own type.","`async fn` in a trait — the same, but with a `Future`.","Such traits aren't dyn compatible."]}],"goals":["Return `impl Trait` from trait methods","Write `async fn` right in a trait"]},{"id":"x02-p","title":"Practice: fine points of traits","min":20,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Plugins: a `Plugin` trait with `name(&self) -> &str` and `run(&self, input: i32) -> i32`, plus a constructor `create() -> Self`. Make the trait dyn compatible, implement two plugins (`Double`: multiplies by 2, `Inc`: adds 1) and run the number 5 through all of them in order.","code":"trait Plugin {\n    fn create() -> Self;\n    fn name(&self) -> &str;\n    fn run(&self, input: i32) -> i32;\n}\n\n// your code: Double and Inc\n\nfn main() {\n    let chain: Vec<Box<dyn Plugin>> = vec![Box::new(Double::create()), Box::new(Inc::create())];\n    let mut x = 5;\n    for p in &chain {\n        x = p.run(x);\n        println!(\"{} -> {x}\", p.name());\n    }\n}","solution":"trait Plugin {\n    fn create() -> Self\n    where\n        Self: Sized;\n    fn name(&self) -> &str;\n    fn run(&self, input: i32) -> i32;\n}\n\nstruct Double;\nstruct Inc;\n\nimpl Plugin for Double {\n    fn create() -> Self { Double }\n    fn name(&self) -> &str { \"double\" }\n    fn run(&self, input: i32) -> i32 { input * 2 }\n}\n\nimpl Plugin for Inc {\n    fn create() -> Self { Inc }\n    fn name(&self) -> &str { \"inc\" }\n    fn run(&self, input: i32) -> i32 { input + 1 }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let chain: Vec<Box<dyn Plugin>> = vec![Box::new(Double::create()), Box::new(Inc::create())];\n    let mut x = 5;\n    for p in &chain {\n        x = p.run(x);\n        println!(\"{} -> {x}\", p.name());\n    }\n}","expect":"double -> 10\ninc -> 11","hints":["`fn create() -> Self where Self: Sized;` — otherwise the trait isn't dyn compatible."]},{"t":"task","md":"An event bus with `Any`: `handle(event: &dyn Any)` prints `click (x, y)` for `Click`, `key K` for `Key`, and `unknown` for anything else.","code":"use std::any::Any;\n\nstruct Click {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\nstruct Key(char);\n\nfn handle(event: &dyn Any) {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let events: Vec<Box<dyn Any>> = vec![Box::new(Click { x: 3, y: 4 }), Box::new(Key('q')), Box::new(42u8)];\n    for e in &events {\n        handle(e.as_ref());\n    }\n}","solution":"use std::any::Any;\n\nstruct Click {\n    x: i32,\n    y: i32,\n}\nstruct Key(char);\n\nfn handle(event: &dyn Any) {\n    if let Some(c) = event.downcast_ref::<Click>() {\n        println!(\"click ({}, {})\", c.x, c.y);\n    } else if let Some(k) = event.downcast_ref::<Key>() {\n        println!(\"key {}\", k.0);\n    } else {\n        println!(\"unknown\");\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let events: Vec<Box<dyn Any>> = vec![Box::new(Click { x: 3, y: 4 }), Box::new(Key('q')), Box::new(42u8)];\n    for e in &events {\n        handle(e.as_ref());\n    }\n}","expect":"click (3, 4)\nkey q\nunknown","hints":["A chain: `if let Some(c) = event.downcast_ref::<Click>() { ... } else if ...`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A `LendingIterator` over chunks of a slice: `ChunksMut` yields `&mut [i32]` of `n` items. Use it to reverse every chunk.","code":"trait LendingIterator {\n    type Item<'a>\n    where\n        Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item<'_>>;\n}\n\nstruct ChunksMut<'s> {\n    data: &'s mut [i32],\n    n: usize,\n    pos: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<'s> LendingIterator for ChunksMut<'s> {\n    type Item<'a> = &'a mut [i32] where Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&mut [i32]> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut v = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\n    let mut it = ChunksMut { data: &mut v, n: 2, pos: 0 };\n    while let Some(c) = it.next() {\n        c.reverse();\n    }\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"trait LendingIterator {\n    type Item<'a>\n    where\n        Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Self::Item<'_>>;\n}\n\nstruct ChunksMut<'s> {\n    data: &'s mut [i32],\n    n: usize,\n    pos: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<'s> LendingIterator for ChunksMut<'s> {\n    type Item<'a> = &'a mut [i32] where Self: 'a;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&mut [i32]> {\n        if self.pos >= self.data.len() {\n            return None;\n        }\n        let start = self.pos;\n        let end = (start + self.n).min(self.data.len());\n        self.pos = end;\n        Some(&mut self.data[start..end])\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut v = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];\n    let mut it = ChunksMut { data: &mut v, n: 2, pos: 0 };\n    while let Some(c) = it.next() {\n        c.reverse();\n    }\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"[2, 1, 4, 3, 5]","hints":["The end of a chunk is `(start + n).min(len)`, so the last chunk doesn't go past the end."]},{"t":"task","md":"A `Source` trait with a method `numbers(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32>` and a default method `max_or_zero`. Implement it for `Range10` (the numbers 1..=10) and `Fixed` (its own vector). Print the maximums.","code":"trait Source {\n    fn numbers(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32>;\n    fn max_or_zero(&self) -> u32 {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Range10;\nstruct Fixed(Vec<u32>);\n\n// your code\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Range10.max_or_zero());\n    println!(\"{}\", Fixed(vec![]).max_or_zero());\n    println!(\"{}\", Fixed(vec![4, 9, 2]).max_or_zero());\n}","solution":"trait Source {\n    fn numbers(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32>;\n    fn max_or_zero(&self) -> u32 {\n        self.numbers().max().unwrap_or(0)\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Range10;\nstruct Fixed(Vec<u32>);\n\nimpl Source for Range10 {\n    fn numbers(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32> {\n        1..=10\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Source for Fixed {\n    fn numbers(&self) -> impl Iterator<Item = u32> {\n        self.0.iter().copied()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", Range10.max_or_zero());\n    println!(\"{}\", Fixed(vec![]).max_or_zero());\n    println!(\"{}\", Fixed(vec![4, 9, 2]).max_or_zero());\n}","expect":"10\n0\n9","hints":["`self.numbers().max().unwrap_or(0)`.","For `Fixed` — `self.0.iter().copied()`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`where Self: Sized` — constructors in dyn-compatible traits.","`downcast_ref` — handling mixed events.","GATs let you change data in chunks through your own iterator.","`impl Iterator` in a trait — flexible data sources."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Use dyn compatibility, upcasting, Any, GATs and RPITIT"]}]},{"id":"x03","title":"Subtyping, variance and fighting the borrow checker","sub":"Why &'static fits where &'a is expected but &mut doesn't, and everyday tricks","lessons":[{"id":"x03-1","title":"Lifetime subtyping","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Long fits where short is expected","md":"Rust has almost no subtyping, except in one place — **lifetimes**. If a reference lives longer, you can pass it where a shorter one is expected.\n\n```\nfn show<'a>(s: &'a str) { println!(\"{s}\"); }\nlet lit: &'static str = \"an eternal string\";\nshow(lit); // &'static str fits where &'a str is expected\n```\nWe say `'static` is a **subtype** of any `'a`. It's written `'static: 'a` — \"`'static` lives at least as long as `'a`\".\n\n> **Analogy:** a yearly pass works where a daily pass is required. Not the other way round."},{"t":"text","title":"How to read 'a: 'b and T: 'a","md":"- `'a: 'b` — \"`'a` lives **at least as long as** `'b`\".\n- `T: 'a` — \"`T` holds no references that die before `'a`\".\n\n```\nstruct Holder<'a, T: 'a> {\n    item: &'a T,\n}\n```\nThese days the compiler often infers such bounds by itself, but they show up in error messages all the time."},{"t":"code","code":"fn pick_first<'long: 'short, 'short>(a: &'long str, _b: &'short str) -> &'short str {\n    a // 'long lives longer, so it fits as 'short\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let outer = String::from(\"outside\");\n    let r;\n    {\n        let inner = String::from(\"inside\");\n        r = pick_first(&outer, &inner);\n        println!(\"{r}\");\n    }\n}","out":"outside","after":"The result is labeled with the short term `'short`, so it can't be used after the block — even though it really points to `outer`. The labels honestly say \"no longer than the short one\"."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `'a: 'b` mean?","options":["`'a` lives at least as long as `'b`","`'a` lives shorter than `'b`","`'a` and `'b` are the same"],"answer":0,"why":["Right.","The opposite.","Not necessarily: `'a` may be longer."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `choose` returns one of two `&str`, and `main` stores its result in a variable that lives longer than the block. Add labels so the code compiles.","code":"fn choose<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str, first: bool) -> &'a str {\n    if first { a } else { b }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let saved: &'static str;\n    {\n        let tmp = String::from(\"temporary\");\n        let _ = choose(&tmp, \"x\", true);\n        saved = choose(\"first\", \"second\", false);\n    }\n    println!(\"{saved}\");\n}","solution":"fn choose<'a>(a: &'a str, b: &'a str, first: bool) -> &'a str {\n    if first { a } else { b }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let saved: &'static str;\n    {\n        let tmp = String::from(\"temporary\");\n        let _ = choose(&tmp, \"x\", true);\n        saved = choose(\"first\", \"second\", false);\n    }\n    println!(\"{saved}\");\n}","expect":"second","hints":["A trap: the code is already correct! In the second call both arguments are `'static`, so `'a = 'static` and the result is `'static` too. Just run it."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A long reference fits where a short one is expected: `'static` fits everywhere.","`'a: 'b` — `'a` lives at least as long as `'b`. `T: 'a` — `T` holds no shorter references.","A result's label is a promise of \"no longer than\", not its real term."]}],"goals":["See that a longer-lived reference fits where a shorter one is expected","Read the bounds `'a: 'b` and `T: 'a`"]},{"id":"x03-2","title":"Variance","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Three kinds","md":"**Variance** answers the question: if `'long` fits where `'short` is expected, does `Type<'long>` fit where `Type<'short>` is expected?\n\n- **Covariance** — yes. `&'a T`, `Box<T>`, `Vec<T>`, `Option<T>`. Almost everything.\n- **Invariance** — no, only an exact match. `&mut T`, `Cell<T>`, `RefCell<T>`, `UnsafeCell<T>`.\n- **Contravariance** — the reverse. It only shows up in function arguments: `fn(T)`.\n\n> **Analogy:** a box with a yearly pass can go to someone expecting a box with a daily pass — they'll only look. But if the box is given so they can **put** something of theirs into it, swapping is dangerous: they'll put a daily pass where you expect a yearly one."},{"t":"text","title":"Why &mut is invariant","md":"If `&mut &'static str` fitted where `&mut &'short str` is expected, you could write a **short** reference where everyone thinks an **eternal** one lives:\n\n```\nfn put<'a>(slot: &mut &'a str, s: &'a str) { *slot = s; }\n\nlet mut forever: &'static str = \"eternal\";\n{\n    let tmp = String::from(\"temporary\");\n    put(&mut forever, &tmp); // error! otherwise forever would point to a dead tmp\n}\n```\nThe invariance of `&mut` closes that hole."},{"t":"code","fails":true,"md":"Run it and look at the error — that's invariance protecting you:","code":"fn put<'a>(slot: &mut &'a str, s: &'a str) {\n    *slot = s;\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut forever: &'static str = \"eternal\";\n    {\n        let tmp = String::from(\"temporary\");\n        put(&mut forever, &tmp);\n    }\n    println!(\"{forever}\");\n}","after":"The compiler says: `tmp` does not live long enough. Through `&mut` you can't \"shorten\" a term, so you can't smuggle a short reference into a long-lived place."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which type is invariant in `T`?","options":["`&'a T`","`Vec<T>`","`Cell<T>`"],"answer":2,"why":["Covariant: read-only.","Covariant.","Right. You can write through a `Cell`, so swapping is dangerous."]},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why can a `Vec<&'static str>` go where a `Vec<&'a str>` is expected, but a `&mut Vec<&'static str>` can't go where a `&mut Vec<&'a str>` is expected?","options":["Through `&mut`, the function could put a short reference into the vector","A `Vec` can't be changed","It's a compiler bug"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. An owned `Vec` is handed over whole — no hole.","It can, but ownership goes to whoever received it.","It's a deliberate safety rule."]},{"t":"task","md":"The function `add_word` puts a reference to a word into a list. The list and the words in `main` live equally long. Add labels so the code compiles.","code":"fn add_word(list: &mut Vec<&str>, w: &str) {\n    list.push(w);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = String::from(\"fish whale crab\");\n    let mut words = Vec::new();\n    for w in text.split(' ') {\n        add_word(&mut words, w);\n    }\n    println!(\"{words:?}\");\n}","solution":"fn add_word<'a>(list: &mut Vec<&'a str>, w: &'a str) {\n    list.push(w);\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = String::from(\"fish whale crab\");\n    let mut words = Vec::new();\n    for w in text.split(' ') {\n        add_word(&mut words, w);\n    }\n    println!(\"{words:?}\");\n}","expect":"[\"fish\", \"whale\", \"crab\"]","hints":["The word goes **inside** the list, so it has the same term as the elements: `list: &mut Vec<&'a str>, w: &'a str`.","The reference to the list itself (`&mut`) doesn't need its own label."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Covariant: `&T`, `Box<T>`, `Vec<T>` — long fits where short is expected.","Invariant: `&mut T`, `Cell<T>` — only an exact match.","Invariance protects against writing a short reference into a long-lived place."]}],"goals":["See where subtyping \"passes through\" a type and where it doesn't","Learn why `&mut` is stricter than `&`"]},{"id":"x03-3","title":"PhantomData and variance","min":7,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"PhantomData decides three things","md":"When a struct has a raw pointer or a parameter that isn't stored, `PhantomData` tells the compiler **how** to treat it:\n\n- `PhantomData<T>` — \"I sort of own a `T`\": covariant, `Send/Sync` like `T`, `T` may be dropped along with it.\n- `PhantomData<&'a T>` — \"I borrow a `T` for `'a`\".\n- `PhantomData<fn() -> T>` — \"I only produce `T`s\": covariant, but doesn't affect `Send/Sync` or dropping.\n- `PhantomData<*const T>` — \"like a raw pointer\": removes `Send` and `Sync`."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::marker::PhantomData;\n\nstruct OwnsT<T>(PhantomData<T>);\nstruct NotThreadSafe(PhantomData<*const u8>);\n\nfn is_send<T: Send>() -> bool { true }\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", is_send::<OwnsT<String>>());\n    // println!(\"{}\", is_send::<NotThreadSafe>()); // error: *const u8 isn't Send\n    let _x = NotThreadSafe(PhantomData);\n    println!(\"sizes: {} {}\", std::mem::size_of::<OwnsT<String>>(), std::mem::size_of::<NotThreadSafe>());\n}","out":"true\nsizes: 0 0","after":"Uncomment the line and see: `PhantomData<*const u8>` made the type unusable across threads. Sometimes that's exactly what you want — say, for a handle that must stay on one thread."},{"t":"quiz","md":"You need to forbid sending a type to another thread without adding data. What goes in the field?","options":["`PhantomData<u8>`","`PhantomData<*const ()>`","`PhantomData<String>`"],"answer":1,"why":["`u8` is `Send`, nothing changes.","Right. Raw pointers are neither `Send` nor `Sync`.","`String` is `Send`."]},{"t":"deep","title":"Drop check","md":"When a type with `Drop` is dropped, the compiler checks that `drop` won't touch data that's already dead. This is the **drop check**.\n\nFor your own collections built on raw pointers, it's important to tell the compiler \"I own `T`\" with `PhantomData<T>`. Then it understands that dropping the collection drops the elements too, and checks the lifetimes correctly.\n\nThe standard library sometimes relaxes the check with a special attribute `#[may_dangle]` (nightly only). Ordinary code doesn't need it — the right `PhantomData` is enough."},{"t":"recap","items":["`PhantomData<T>` — I own; `<&'a T>` — I borrow; `<fn() -> T>` — I only produce.","`PhantomData<*const T>` removes `Send` and `Sync`.","`PhantomData` always has size zero."]}],"goals":["Choose the right kind of `PhantomData`","Know how it affects `Send`, `Sync` and dropping"]},{"id":"x03-4","title":"Tricks against fighting the borrow checker","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Trick 1: borrow fields separately","md":"The compiler tells a struct's **fields** apart. `&mut self.a` and `&self.b` at the same time — fine. But taking the whole `&mut self` through a method — not fine.\n\n```\n// bad: the method borrows all of self\nlet first = self.first_item();\nself.counter += 1; // error if first is still alive\n// good: separate fields\nlet first = &self.items[0];\nself.counter += 1; // ok\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"Trick 2: an index instead of a reference","md":"Don't keep a reference to an element — keep its **index**. An index is just a number; it borrows nothing."},{"t":"text","title":"Trick 3: entry instead of get + insert","md":"```\n// bad\nif !map.contains_key(&k) { map.insert(k, 0); }\n*map.get_mut(&k).unwrap() += 1;\n// good\n*map.entry(k).or_insert(0) += 1;\n```"},{"t":"text","title":"Trick 4: read first, then change","md":"Pull the value you need into a variable (copy a number, `clone` a small string), end the borrow — and only then change things.\n\n> **Analogy:** don't try to read a book and re-glue its pages at the same time. Copy the line you need onto a note, close the book — then glue."},{"t":"task","md":"The `promote_first` method must bump the promotion counter and set the `boss` flag for the first employee. Right now it doesn't compile. Fix it by borrowing fields separately.","code":"struct Team {\n    names: Vec<String>,\n    bosses: Vec<bool>,\n    promotions: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Team {\n    fn first_name(&self) -> &String {\n        &self.names[0]\n    }\n    fn promote_first(&mut self) -> String {\n        let name = self.first_name();\n        self.bosses[0] = true;\n        self.promotions += 1;\n        format!(\"{name} is the boss now\")\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut t = Team { names: vec![String::from(\"Ada\")], bosses: vec![false], promotions: 0 };\n    println!(\"{}\", t.promote_first());\n    println!(\"{} {}\", t.bosses[0], t.promotions);\n}","solution":"struct Team {\n    names: Vec<String>,\n    bosses: Vec<bool>,\n    promotions: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Team {\n    fn promote_first(&mut self) -> String {\n        let name = &self.names[0];\n        self.bosses[0] = true;\n        self.promotions += 1;\n        format!(\"{name} is the boss now\")\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut t = Team { names: vec![String::from(\"Ada\")], bosses: vec![false], promotions: 0 };\n    println!(\"{}\", t.promote_first());\n    println!(\"{} {}\", t.bosses[0], t.promotions);\n}","expect":"Ada is the boss now\ntrue 1","hints":["`self.first_name()` borrows all of `self`. Take the field directly: `let name = &self.names[0];`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Find the index of the longest word and **append** `!` to it right in the vector. You can't hold a reference — use the index.","code":"fn main() {\n    let mut words = vec![String::from(\"cat\"), String::from(\"hippo\"), String::from(\"ox\")];\n    // your code\n    println!(\"{words:?}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let mut words = vec![String::from(\"cat\"), String::from(\"hippo\"), String::from(\"ox\")];\n    let idx = (0..words.len()).max_by_key(|&i| words[i].chars().count()).unwrap();\n    words[idx].push('!');\n    println!(\"{words:?}\");\n}","expect":"[\"cat\", \"hippo!\", \"ox\"]","hints":["`(0..words.len()).max_by_key(|&i| words[i].chars().count())` returns the index.","Then `words[idx].push('!')` — the borrow used for searching is already over."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Separate fields — fine; all of `self` through a method — not fine.","An index instead of a reference.","`entry` instead of `get` + `insert`.","Read first and end the borrow, then change."]}],"goals":["Know four common tricks that make borrow errors go away","Solve tasks without extra `clone()`s"]},{"id":"x03-p","title":"Practice: borrowing","min":20,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Count letter frequencies in a text into a `BTreeMap<char, u32>` using `entry`. Skip spaces.","code":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"banana bar\";\n    let mut freq: BTreeMap<char, u32> = BTreeMap::new();\n    // your code\n    println!(\"{freq:?}\");\n}","solution":"use std::collections::BTreeMap;\n\nfn main() {\n    let text = \"banana bar\";\n    let mut freq: BTreeMap<char, u32> = BTreeMap::new();\n    for c in text.chars().filter(|c| !c.is_whitespace()) {\n        *freq.entry(c).or_insert(0) += 1;\n    }\n    println!(\"{freq:?}\");\n}","expect":"{'a': 4, 'b': 2, 'n': 2, 'r': 1}","hints":["`*freq.entry(c).or_insert(0) += 1;`."]},{"t":"task","md":"The `transfer` method moves money between two accounts in one vector. You can't directly get two `&mut` to elements of the same vector — use `split_at_mut` or work by indexes.","code":"struct Bank {\n    accounts: Vec<i64>,\n}\n\nimpl Bank {\n    fn transfer(&mut self, from: usize, to: usize, amount: i64) {\n        let a = &mut self.accounts[from];\n        let b = &mut self.accounts[to];\n        *a -= amount;\n        *b += amount;\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut bank = Bank { accounts: vec![100, 50, 0] };\n    bank.transfer(0, 2, 30);\n    bank.transfer(1, 0, 10);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", bank.accounts);\n}","solution":"struct Bank {\n    accounts: Vec<i64>,\n}\n\nimpl Bank {\n    fn transfer(&mut self, from: usize, to: usize, amount: i64) {\n        self.accounts[from] -= amount;\n        self.accounts[to] += amount;\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut bank = Bank { accounts: vec![100, 50, 0] };\n    bank.transfer(0, 2, 30);\n    bank.transfer(1, 0, 10);\n    println!(\"{:?}\", bank.accounts);\n}","expect":"[80, 40, 30]","hints":["Simplest: don't hold two references: `self.accounts[from] -= amount; self.accounts[to] += amount;`.","If you really need two `&mut` at once — `get_disjoint_mut([from, to])` or `split_at_mut`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A cache: `get_or_compute` returns a `&str` from the map, and if the key is missing it computes the value (the key in uppercase) and stores it. Fix the version that doesn't compile.","code":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nstruct Cache {\n    map: HashMap<String, String>,\n    misses: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Cache {\n    fn get_or_compute(&mut self, key: &str) -> &str {\n        if let Some(v) = self.map.get(key) {\n            return v;\n        }\n        self.misses += 1;\n        self.map.insert(key.to_string(), key.to_uppercase());\n        self.map.get(key).unwrap()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = Cache { map: HashMap::new(), misses: 0 };\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get_or_compute(\"rust\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get_or_compute(\"rust\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get_or_compute(\"crab\"));\n    println!(\"misses: {}\", c.misses);\n}","solution":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nstruct Cache {\n    map: HashMap<String, String>,\n    misses: u32,\n}\n\nimpl Cache {\n    fn get_or_compute(&mut self, key: &str) -> &str {\n        if !self.map.contains_key(key) {\n            self.misses += 1;\n        }\n        self.map.entry(key.to_string()).or_insert_with(|| key.to_uppercase())\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut c = Cache { map: HashMap::new(), misses: 0 };\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get_or_compute(\"rust\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get_or_compute(\"rust\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", c.get_or_compute(\"crab\"));\n    println!(\"misses: {}\", c.misses);\n}","expect":"RUST\nRUST\nCRAB\nmisses: 2","hints":["This is a known limitation of the checker: the early `return v` from `get` keeps the borrow alive until the end of the function.","Split it into \"check\" (`contains_key`) and \"insert or take\" (`entry(...).or_insert_with(...)`)."]},{"t":"task","mustContain":["-> &str"],"md":"The `longest_line` function returns the longest line of a text. Right now it takes a `&String` and returns a `String` via `clone`. Rewrite it with no copies: take `&str`, return `&str`.","code":"fn longest_line(text: &String) -> String {\n    let mut best = String::new();\n    for line in text.lines() {\n        if line.len() > best.len() {\n            best = line.to_string();\n        }\n    }\n    best.clone()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let t = String::from(\"short\\nthe longest line here\\nmedium\");\n    println!(\"{}\", longest_line(&t));\n}","solution":"fn longest_line(text: &str) -> &str {\n    let mut best = \"\";\n    for line in text.lines() {\n        if line.len() > best.len() {\n            best = line;\n        }\n    }\n    best\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let t = String::from(\"short\\nthe longest line here\\nmedium\");\n    println!(\"{}\", longest_line(&t));\n}","expect":"the longest line here","hints":["One reference in — no labels needed: `fn longest_line(text: &str) -> &str`.","`let mut best = \"\";` and assign `best = line;` — they're all pieces of the original text."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`entry` — counts and caches without a double lookup.","Indexes — when you need to change two elements of one collection.","An early `return` of a reference is a common trap; `entry` saves the day.","`&str` instead of `String` — fewer copies."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Fix several typical borrow errors without extra copies"]}]},{"id":"x04","title":"Your own async","sub":"Futures by hand, a real Waker, a mini executor, Pin and async closures","lessons":[{"id":"x04-1","title":"A Future by hand","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"The contract again","md":"`Future` has one method:\n\n```\nfn poll(self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Self::Output>;\n```\n- done — `Poll::Ready(value)`;\n- not done — `Poll::Pending`, **and** a promise to wake the executor when it's worth polling again.\n\nThe promise is kept through `cx.waker()`. If you return `Pending` and wake nobody, an honest executor **never comes back** — the task hangs forever.\n\n> **Analogy:** you call support and they say \"we'll call you back\". If they don't write down your number (the Waker), the call never comes."},{"t":"code","md":"A Future that says \"not yet\" three times and each time immediately asks to be polled again:","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::{pin, Pin};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nstruct Countdown(u32);\n\nimpl Future for Countdown {\n    type Output = &'static str;\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<&'static str> {\n        if self.0 == 0 {\n            Poll::Ready(\"liftoff!\")\n        } else {\n            println!(\"{} left\", self.0);\n            self.0 -= 1;\n            cx.waker().wake_by_ref(); // \"poll me again\"\n            Poll::Pending\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", block_on(Countdown(3)));\n}","out":"3 left\n2 left\n1 left\nliftoff!","after":"`mut self: Pin<&mut Self>` — you can change fields because `Countdown` is `Unpin` (it holds no references to itself). More in the Pin lesson."},{"t":"quiz","md":"A Future returned `Poll::Pending` and neither stored nor called the Waker. What happens in a real executor like tokio?","options":["The executor polls it again in a second","The task hangs: nobody will ever wake it","A compile error"],"answer":1,"why":["Executors don't poll \"just in case\".","Right.","The compiler doesn't check this."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write `YieldNow`: on the first poll it returns `Pending` and wakes itself right away; on the second it returns `Ready(())`. That's \"let other tasks go first\".","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::{pin, Pin};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nstruct YieldNow {\n    yielded: bool,\n}\n\nimpl Future for YieldNow {\n    type Output = ();\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<()> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> (F::Output, u32) {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    let mut polls = 0;\n    loop {\n        polls += 1;\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return (v, polls);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let (_, polls) = block_on(async {\n        YieldNow { yielded: false }.await;\n        YieldNow { yielded: false }.await;\n    });\n    println!(\"polls: {polls}\");\n}","solution":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::{pin, Pin};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nstruct YieldNow {\n    yielded: bool,\n}\n\nimpl Future for YieldNow {\n    type Output = ();\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<()> {\n        if self.yielded {\n            Poll::Ready(())\n        } else {\n            self.yielded = true;\n            cx.waker().wake_by_ref();\n            Poll::Pending\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> (F::Output, u32) {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    let mut polls = 0;\n    loop {\n        polls += 1;\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return (v, polls);\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let (_, polls) = block_on(async {\n        YieldNow { yielded: false }.await;\n        YieldNow { yielded: false }.await;\n    });\n    println!(\"polls: {polls}\");\n}","expect":"polls: 3","hints":["If `self.yielded` — `Ready(())`. Otherwise set the flag, call `cx.waker().wake_by_ref()` and return `Pending`.","Two `YieldNow`s → two `Pending`s → the third poll finishes everything."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`impl Future`: `type Output` + `fn poll`.","`Pending` only together with a promise to wake through `cx.waker()`.","`wake_by_ref()` — \"poll me again\"."]}],"goals":["Implement the `Future` trait for your own struct","See why a Future \"wakes\" itself"]},{"id":"x04-2","title":"A real Waker","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Don't spin for nothing","md":"Our old `block_on` polled the Future in an endless loop. If we wait for a one-second timer, the CPU spins uselessly for a whole second.\n\nThe right way: got `Pending` — **go to sleep**. Wake up when someone calls `wake()`.\n\nThe standard library has everything needed:\n- `std::task::Wake` — a trait: \"what to do on wake-up\";\n- `Waker::from(Arc<T>)` — make a Waker from your own struct;\n- `thread::park()` / `thread.unpark()` — sleep and wake a thread.\n\n> **Analogy:** don't stand at the stove staring at the pot; set a timer and lie down. The timer wakes you."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::{pin, Pin};\nuse std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Wake, Waker};\nuse std::thread::{self, Thread};\nuse std::time::Duration;\n\nstruct ThreadWaker(Thread);\n\nimpl Wake for ThreadWaker {\n    fn wake(self: Arc<Self>) {\n        self.0.unpark();\n    }\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let waker = Waker::from(Arc::new(ThreadWaker(thread::current())));\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(&waker);\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n        thread::park(); // sleep until called\n    }\n}\n\n// A timer: on the first poll it starts a thread that wakes the task later\nstruct Sleep {\n    dur: Duration,\n    state: Arc<Mutex<(bool, Option<Waker>)>>, // (done?, whom to wake)\n    started: bool,\n}\n\nimpl Future for Sleep {\n    type Output = ();\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<()> {\n        let mut st = self.state.lock().unwrap();\n        if st.0 {\n            return Poll::Ready(());\n        }\n        st.1 = Some(cx.waker().clone());\n        drop(st);\n        if !self.started {\n            self.started = true;\n            let (dur, state) = (self.dur, Arc::clone(&self.state));\n            thread::spawn(move || {\n                thread::sleep(dur);\n                let mut st = state.lock().unwrap();\n                st.0 = true;\n                if let Some(w) = st.1.take() {\n                    w.wake();\n                }\n            });\n        }\n        Poll::Pending\n    }\n}\n\nfn sleep(ms: u64) -> Sleep {\n    Sleep { dur: Duration::from_millis(ms), state: Arc::new(Mutex::new((false, None))), started: false }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    block_on(async {\n        println!(\"setting a timer\");\n        sleep(50).await;\n        println!(\"woke up\");\n    });\n}","out":"setting a timer\nwoke up","after":"While the timer runs, the main thread sleeps in `park()` and uses no CPU."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does `Waker::from(Arc::new(ThreadWaker(...)))` do?","options":["Makes a Waker that calls our `Wake::wake` method on `wake()`","Starts a new thread","Wakes the current thread right away"],"answer":0,"why":["Right.","No thread is started here.","It wakes only when `wake()` is called."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write `Wake` for `CountingWaker`: every `wake()` (and `wake_by_ref()`) adds 1 to an `AtomicUsize` counter. Test it with `YieldNow` called three times.","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::{pin, Pin};\nuse std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};\nuse std::sync::Arc;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Wake, Waker};\n\nstruct CountingWaker(AtomicUsize);\n\n// your code: impl Wake for CountingWaker\n\nstruct YieldNow(bool);\n\nimpl Future for YieldNow {\n    type Output = ();\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<()> {\n        if self.0 {\n            return Poll::Ready(());\n        }\n        self.0 = true;\n        cx.waker().wake_by_ref();\n        Poll::Pending\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let counter = Arc::new(CountingWaker(AtomicUsize::new(0)));\n    let waker = Waker::from(Arc::clone(&counter));\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(&waker);\n    let mut fut = pin!(async {\n        for _ in 0..3 {\n            YieldNow(false).await;\n        }\n    });\n    while fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx).is_pending() {}\n    println!(\"woken: {}\", counter.0.load(Ordering::SeqCst));\n}","solution":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::{pin, Pin};\nuse std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};\nuse std::sync::Arc;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Wake, Waker};\n\nstruct CountingWaker(AtomicUsize);\n\nimpl Wake for CountingWaker {\n    fn wake(self: Arc<Self>) {\n        self.0.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);\n    }\n    fn wake_by_ref(self: &Arc<Self>) {\n        self.0.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst);\n    }\n}\n\nstruct YieldNow(bool);\n\nimpl Future for YieldNow {\n    type Output = ();\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<()> {\n        if self.0 {\n            return Poll::Ready(());\n        }\n        self.0 = true;\n        cx.waker().wake_by_ref();\n        Poll::Pending\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let counter = Arc::new(CountingWaker(AtomicUsize::new(0)));\n    let waker = Waker::from(Arc::clone(&counter));\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(&waker);\n    let mut fut = pin!(async {\n        for _ in 0..3 {\n            YieldNow(false).await;\n        }\n    });\n    while fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx).is_pending() {}\n    println!(\"woken: {}\", counter.0.load(Ordering::SeqCst));\n}","expect":"woken: 3","hints":["`impl Wake for CountingWaker { fn wake(self: Arc<Self>) { self.0.fetch_add(1, Ordering::SeqCst); } }`.","By default `wake_by_ref` clones the `Arc` and calls `wake` — you can skip it, but your own version is cheaper."]},{"t":"deep","title":"How real executors are built","md":"tokio and other executors do the same, just at a larger scale:\n- a **reactor** — one thread (or a system mechanism like epoll/kqueue/IOCP) watches sockets and timers and calls `wake()` when something is ready;\n- a **scheduler** — a queue of tasks ready to be polled; worker threads take tasks from it;\n- **work stealing** — an idle thread takes tasks from a busy one.\n\nA Waker in tokio is essentially \"put this task back into the queue\". That's exactly what we'll build in the next lesson."},{"t":"recap","items":["`impl Wake` + `Waker::from(Arc<..>)` — your own Waker.","`thread::park` / `unpark` — sleep until woken.","A timer stores the Waker and calls `wake()` when time is up."]}],"goals":["Make a Waker from your own struct with the `Wake` trait","Write a `block_on` that sleeps until it's woken"]},{"id":"x04-3","title":"A mini task executor","min":10,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"How it's built","md":"1. A **task** — a boxed Future plus a reference to the queue.\n2. The **queue** — an `mpsc` channel: tasks ready to be polled go into it.\n3. The task's **Waker** — on `wake()` it puts **this** task back into the queue.\n4. The **loop** — take a task from the queue, poll it. `Pending` — it'll come back by itself when woken. `Ready` — done.\n\n> **Analogy:** a cashier and a line. A customer who has to fetch a forgotten item steps away. When they're back (wake), they rejoin the line."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::Pin;\nuse std::sync::mpsc::{channel, Receiver, Sender};\nuse std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Wake, Waker};\n\ntype BoxFuture = Pin<Box<dyn Future<Output = ()> + Send>>;\n\nstruct Task {\n    fut: Mutex<Option<BoxFuture>>,\n    queue: Sender<Arc<Task>>,\n}\n\nimpl Wake for Task {\n    fn wake(self: Arc<Self>) {\n        let q = self.queue.clone();\n        q.send(self).unwrap(); // put the task back into the queue\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Executor {\n    tx: Sender<Arc<Task>>,\n    rx: Receiver<Arc<Task>>,\n    alive: usize,\n}\n\nimpl Executor {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        let (tx, rx) = channel();\n        Executor { tx, rx, alive: 0 }\n    }\n    fn spawn(&mut self, f: impl Future<Output = ()> + Send + 'static) {\n        self.alive += 1;\n        let task = Arc::new(Task { fut: Mutex::new(Some(Box::pin(f))), queue: self.tx.clone() });\n        self.tx.send(task).unwrap();\n    }\n    fn run(&mut self) {\n        while self.alive > 0 {\n            let task = self.rx.recv().unwrap();\n            let mut slot = task.fut.lock().unwrap();\n            if let Some(mut f) = slot.take() {\n                let waker = Waker::from(Arc::clone(&task));\n                let mut cx = Context::from_waker(&waker);\n                if f.as_mut().poll(&mut cx).is_pending() {\n                    *slot = Some(f);\n                } else {\n                    self.alive -= 1;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct YieldNow(bool);\nimpl Future for YieldNow {\n    type Output = ();\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<()> {\n        if self.0 {\n            return Poll::Ready(());\n        }\n        self.0 = true;\n        cx.waker().wake_by_ref();\n        Poll::Pending\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut ex = Executor::new();\n    for name in [\"A\", \"B\"] {\n        ex.spawn(async move {\n            for step in 1..=3 {\n                println!(\"{name}{step}\");\n                YieldNow(false).await;\n            }\n        });\n    }\n    ex.run();\n    println!(\"all tasks done\");\n}","out":"A1\nB1\nA2\nB2\nA3\nB3\nall tasks done","after":"The tasks **take turns** yielding to each other on one thread. That's cooperative multitasking — this is how async works."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does a task's `wake()` do in our executor?","options":["Polls the Future right away","Puts the task back into the channel queue","Deletes the task"],"answer":1,"why":["No, it only queues the task.","Right. The `run` loop will poll it when it gets there.","A task is dropped after `Ready`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Add a poll counter to the executor: a `polls: usize` field that grows on every `poll` call; print it after `run`. Two tasks of 2 steps each, with yields.","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::Pin;\nuse std::sync::mpsc::{channel, Receiver, Sender};\nuse std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Wake, Waker};\n\ntype BoxFuture = Pin<Box<dyn Future<Output = ()> + Send>>;\n\nstruct Task {\n    fut: Mutex<Option<BoxFuture>>,\n    queue: Sender<Arc<Task>>,\n}\n\nimpl Wake for Task {\n    fn wake(self: Arc<Self>) {\n        let q = self.queue.clone();\n        q.send(self).unwrap();\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Executor {\n    tx: Sender<Arc<Task>>,\n    rx: Receiver<Arc<Task>>,\n    alive: usize,\n    // add a polls field\n}\n\nimpl Executor {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        let (tx, rx) = channel();\n        Executor { tx, rx, alive: 0 }\n    }\n    fn spawn(&mut self, f: impl Future<Output = ()> + Send + 'static) {\n        self.alive += 1;\n        let task = Arc::new(Task { fut: Mutex::new(Some(Box::pin(f))), queue: self.tx.clone() });\n        self.tx.send(task).unwrap();\n    }\n    fn run(&mut self) {\n        while self.alive > 0 {\n            let task = self.rx.recv().unwrap();\n            let mut slot = task.fut.lock().unwrap();\n            if let Some(mut f) = slot.take() {\n                let waker = Waker::from(Arc::clone(&task));\n                let mut cx = Context::from_waker(&waker);\n                if f.as_mut().poll(&mut cx).is_pending() {\n                    *slot = Some(f);\n                } else {\n                    self.alive -= 1;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct YieldNow(bool);\nimpl Future for YieldNow {\n    type Output = ();\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<()> {\n        if self.0 {\n            return Poll::Ready(());\n        }\n        self.0 = true;\n        cx.waker().wake_by_ref();\n        Poll::Pending\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut ex = Executor::new();\n    for _ in 0..2 {\n        ex.spawn(async {\n            YieldNow(false).await;\n            YieldNow(false).await;\n        });\n    }\n    ex.run();\n    println!(\"polls: {}\", ex.polls);\n}","solution":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::Pin;\nuse std::sync::mpsc::{channel, Receiver, Sender};\nuse std::sync::{Arc, Mutex};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Wake, Waker};\n\ntype BoxFuture = Pin<Box<dyn Future<Output = ()> + Send>>;\n\nstruct Task {\n    fut: Mutex<Option<BoxFuture>>,\n    queue: Sender<Arc<Task>>,\n}\n\nimpl Wake for Task {\n    fn wake(self: Arc<Self>) {\n        let q = self.queue.clone();\n        q.send(self).unwrap();\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Executor {\n    tx: Sender<Arc<Task>>,\n    rx: Receiver<Arc<Task>>,\n    alive: usize,\n    polls: usize,\n}\n\nimpl Executor {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        let (tx, rx) = channel();\n        Executor { tx, rx, alive: 0, polls: 0 }\n    }\n    fn spawn(&mut self, f: impl Future<Output = ()> + Send + 'static) {\n        self.alive += 1;\n        let task = Arc::new(Task { fut: Mutex::new(Some(Box::pin(f))), queue: self.tx.clone() });\n        self.tx.send(task).unwrap();\n    }\n    fn run(&mut self) {\n        while self.alive > 0 {\n            let task = self.rx.recv().unwrap();\n            let mut slot = task.fut.lock().unwrap();\n            if let Some(mut f) = slot.take() {\n                let waker = Waker::from(Arc::clone(&task));\n                let mut cx = Context::from_waker(&waker);\n                self.polls += 1;\n                if f.as_mut().poll(&mut cx).is_pending() {\n                    *slot = Some(f);\n                } else {\n                    self.alive -= 1;\n                }\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nstruct YieldNow(bool);\nimpl Future for YieldNow {\n    type Output = ();\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<()> {\n        if self.0 {\n            return Poll::Ready(());\n        }\n        self.0 = true;\n        cx.waker().wake_by_ref();\n        Poll::Pending\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut ex = Executor::new();\n    for _ in 0..2 {\n        ex.spawn(async {\n            YieldNow(false).await;\n            YieldNow(false).await;\n        });\n    }\n    ex.run();\n    println!(\"polls: {}\", ex.polls);\n}","expect":"polls: 6","hints":["Add `polls: usize` to the struct and `polls: 0` in `new`.","Increment `self.polls += 1;` right before `f.as_mut().poll(...)`. Each task: 2 yields + a final poll = 3."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A task = a boxed Future + a reference to the queue.","The task's Waker puts it back into the queue.","The executor loop: take, poll, put back or finish."]}],"goals":["Write an executor that runs many tasks on one thread","See how a Waker puts a task back into the queue"]},{"id":"x04-4","title":"Pin, Unpin and async closures","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Why async blocks can't be moved","md":"An `async` block becomes a **state machine**: a struct that stores all its local variables between `.await`s. If one variable is a reference to another in the same struct, you get a struct that **refers to itself**.\n\nSuch a struct can't be moved in memory: the reference inside would keep pointing at the old place. `Pin` is a promise: \"once pinned, I won't move it\".\n\n> **Analogy:** a tent with pegs. While the pegs are in (Pin), you don't drag the tent around, or the ropes end up hanging in the air."},{"t":"text","title":"Unpin","md":"Most types **can** be moved safely even when pinned: numbers, `String`, `Vec`, our `Countdown`. They implement the auto trait `Unpin`, and `Pin` forbids nothing for them.\n\nWays to pin:\n- `std::pin::pin!(fut)` — on the stack, in the current function;\n- `Box::pin(fut)` — on the heap, can be stored and passed around;\n- `Pin::new(&mut x)` — only for `Unpin` types."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::Pin;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nfn is_unpin<T: Unpin>(_: &T) -> bool { true }\n\nfn main() {\n    let n = 5;\n    println!(\"i32 Unpin: {}\", is_unpin(&n));\n\n    let fut = async { 40 + 2 };\n    // is_unpin(&fut); // error: an async block isn't Unpin\n    let mut boxed: Pin<Box<dyn Future<Output = i32>>> = Box::pin(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    if let Poll::Ready(v) = boxed.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n        println!(\"done: {v}\");\n    }\n}","out":"i32 Unpin: true\ndone: 42"},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why does polling an async block need `Pin`?","options":["It may refer to its own fields, so it can't be moved after the first poll","It's too big for the stack","It's faster"],"answer":0,"why":["Right.","Size has nothing to do with it.","Pin is about safety, not speed."]},{"t":"text","title":"Async closures","md":"A closure can be `async` too. You write `async |x| ...`, and accept it with the traits `AsyncFn`, `AsyncFnMut`, `AsyncFnOnce`:\n\n```\nasync fn twice<F: AsyncFn(i32) -> i32>(f: F) -> i32 {\n    f(1).await + f(2).await\n}\n```\nBefore, you wrote `F: Fn(i32) -> Fut, Fut: Future<Output = i32>` — long, and it didn't work if the closure borrowed something from itself. Async closures fixed that."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::pin;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn twice<F: AsyncFn(i32) -> i32>(f: F) -> i32 {\n    f(1).await + f(2).await\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let bonus = 100;\n    let r = block_on(twice(async |x| x * 10 + bonus));\n    println!(\"{r}\");\n}","out":"230","after":"(1·10 + 100) + (2·10 + 100) = 230."},{"t":"task","md":"Write `retry`: it calls an async closure up to `n` times until it returns `Ok`. It returns the first `Ok` or the last error.","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::pin;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\nuse std::cell::Cell;\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn retry<F: AsyncFn() -> Result<u32, String>>(n: u32, f: F) -> Result<u32, String> {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let tries = Cell::new(0);\n    let r = block_on(retry(5, async || {\n        tries.set(tries.get() + 1);\n        if tries.get() < 3 { Err(format!(\"attempt {}\", tries.get())) } else { Ok(tries.get() * 10) }\n    }));\n    println!(\"{r:?} after {} tries\", tries.get());\n    let bad = block_on(retry(2, async || Err::<u32, _>(String::from(\"network down\"))));\n    println!(\"{bad:?}\");\n}","solution":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::pin;\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\nuse std::cell::Cell;\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nasync fn retry<F: AsyncFn() -> Result<u32, String>>(n: u32, f: F) -> Result<u32, String> {\n    let mut last = Err(String::from(\"no attempts\"));\n    for _ in 0..n {\n        last = f().await;\n        if last.is_ok() {\n            break;\n        }\n    }\n    last\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let tries = Cell::new(0);\n    let r = block_on(retry(5, async || {\n        tries.set(tries.get() + 1);\n        if tries.get() < 3 { Err(format!(\"attempt {}\", tries.get())) } else { Ok(tries.get() * 10) }\n    }));\n    println!(\"{r:?} after {} tries\", tries.get());\n    let bad = block_on(retry(2, async || Err::<u32, _>(String::from(\"network down\"))));\n    println!(\"{bad:?}\");\n}","expect":"Ok(30) after 3 tries\nErr(\"network down\")","hints":["Keep the last result in `last`. In the loop `last = f().await;` and stop on `Ok`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["An async block is a state machine that may refer to itself. Hence `Pin`.","`Unpin` — the type can be moved; `Pin` doesn't get in its way.","`pin!` — on the stack, `Box::pin` — on the heap.","`async |x| ...` + `AsyncFn` — async closures."]}],"goals":["See why Futures are pinned in place","Use async closures `async |x| ...`"]},{"id":"x04-p","title":"Practice: your own async","min":25,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"`Ready<T>`: a Future that returns a ready value right away. Keep it in an `Option` and take it on poll with `take()`.","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::{pin, Pin};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nstruct Ready<T>(Option<T>);\n\nimpl<T: Unpin> Future for Ready<T> {\n    type Output = T;\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, _cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<T> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v = block_on(async {\n        let a = Ready(Some(20)).await;\n        let b = Ready(Some(22)).await;\n        a + b\n    });\n    println!(\"{v}\");\n}","solution":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::{pin, Pin};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nstruct Ready<T>(Option<T>);\n\nimpl<T: Unpin> Future for Ready<T> {\n    type Output = T;\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, _cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<T> {\n        Poll::Ready(self.0.take().expect(\"Ready polled after completion\"))\n    }\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v = block_on(async {\n        let a = Ready(Some(20)).await;\n        let b = Ready(Some(22)).await;\n        a + b\n    });\n    println!(\"{v}\");\n}","expect":"42","hints":["`Poll::Ready(self.0.take().unwrap())`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Your own `join`: `Join2` polls two Futures until both finish and returns the pair of results. The Futures sit in `Pin<Box<...>>`, the results in `Option`s.","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::{pin, Pin};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nstruct YieldN(u32);\nimpl Future for YieldN {\n    type Output = u32;\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<u32> {\n        if self.0 == 0 {\n            return Poll::Ready(7);\n        }\n        self.0 -= 1;\n        cx.waker().wake_by_ref();\n        Poll::Pending\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Join2<A: Future, B: Future> {\n    a: Pin<Box<A>>,\n    b: Pin<Box<B>>,\n    ra: Option<A::Output>,\n    rb: Option<B::Output>,\n}\n\nimpl<A: Future, B: Future> Future for Join2<A, B>\nwhere\n    A::Output: Unpin,\n    B::Output: Unpin,\n{\n    type Output = (A::Output, B::Output);\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Self::Output> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn join2<A: Future, B: Future>(a: A, b: B) -> Join2<A, B> {\n    Join2 { a: Box::pin(a), b: Box::pin(b), ra: None, rb: None }\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let (x, y) = block_on(join2(YieldN(1), async { 35 }));\n    println!(\"{x} + {y} = {}\", x + y);\n}","solution":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::{pin, Pin};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nstruct YieldN(u32);\nimpl Future for YieldN {\n    type Output = u32;\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<u32> {\n        if self.0 == 0 {\n            return Poll::Ready(7);\n        }\n        self.0 -= 1;\n        cx.waker().wake_by_ref();\n        Poll::Pending\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Join2<A: Future, B: Future> {\n    a: Pin<Box<A>>,\n    b: Pin<Box<B>>,\n    ra: Option<A::Output>,\n    rb: Option<B::Output>,\n}\n\nimpl<A: Future, B: Future> Future for Join2<A, B>\nwhere\n    A::Output: Unpin,\n    B::Output: Unpin,\n{\n    type Output = (A::Output, B::Output);\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Self::Output> {\n        let this = &mut *self;\n        if this.ra.is_none() {\n            if let Poll::Ready(v) = this.a.as_mut().poll(cx) {\n                this.ra = Some(v);\n            }\n        }\n        if this.rb.is_none() {\n            if let Poll::Ready(v) = this.b.as_mut().poll(cx) {\n                this.rb = Some(v);\n            }\n        }\n        if this.ra.is_some() && this.rb.is_some() {\n            Poll::Ready((this.ra.take().unwrap(), this.rb.take().unwrap()))\n        } else {\n            Poll::Pending\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn join2<A: Future, B: Future>(a: A, b: B) -> Join2<A, B> {\n    Join2 { a: Box::pin(a), b: Box::pin(b), ra: None, rb: None }\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let (x, y) = block_on(join2(YieldN(1), async { 35 }));\n    println!(\"{x} + {y} = {}\", x + y);\n}","expect":"7 + 35 = 42","hints":["`Join2` is `Unpin` (boxed fields and `Option`s with `Unpin` results), so you can take `let this = &mut *self;`.","Poll `a` only while `ra` is still `None`; the same for `b`. Both ready — return the pair via `take()`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A limited Future: `Limited` polls the inner Future at most `max` times. Didn't make it — `Err(\"limit\")`.","code":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::{pin, Pin};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nstruct YieldN(u32);\nimpl Future for YieldN {\n    type Output = &'static str;\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<&'static str> {\n        if self.0 == 0 {\n            return Poll::Ready(\"done\");\n        }\n        self.0 -= 1;\n        cx.waker().wake_by_ref();\n        Poll::Pending\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Limited<F> {\n    inner: Pin<Box<F>>,\n    left: u32,\n}\n\nimpl<F: Future> Future for Limited<F> {\n    type Output = Result<F::Output, &'static str>;\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Self::Output> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", block_on(Limited { inner: Box::pin(YieldN(2)), left: 5 }));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", block_on(Limited { inner: Box::pin(YieldN(10)), left: 5 }));\n}","solution":"use std::future::Future;\nuse std::pin::{pin, Pin};\nuse std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};\n\nstruct YieldN(u32);\nimpl Future for YieldN {\n    type Output = &'static str;\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<&'static str> {\n        if self.0 == 0 {\n            return Poll::Ready(\"done\");\n        }\n        self.0 -= 1;\n        cx.waker().wake_by_ref();\n        Poll::Pending\n    }\n}\n\nstruct Limited<F> {\n    inner: Pin<Box<F>>,\n    left: u32,\n}\n\nimpl<F: Future> Future for Limited<F> {\n    type Output = Result<F::Output, &'static str>;\n    fn poll(mut self: Pin<&mut Self>, cx: &mut Context<'_>) -> Poll<Self::Output> {\n        if self.left == 0 {\n            return Poll::Ready(Err(\"limit\"));\n        }\n        self.left -= 1;\n        match self.inner.as_mut().poll(cx) {\n            Poll::Ready(v) => Poll::Ready(Ok(v)),\n            Poll::Pending => Poll::Pending,\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn block_on<F: Future>(fut: F) -> F::Output {\n    let mut fut = pin!(fut);\n    let mut cx = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());\n    loop {\n        if let Poll::Ready(v) = fut.as_mut().poll(&mut cx) {\n            return v;\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", block_on(Limited { inner: Box::pin(YieldN(2)), left: 5 }));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", block_on(Limited { inner: Box::pin(YieldN(10)), left: 5 }));\n}","expect":"Ok(\"done\")\nErr(\"limit\")","hints":["If `left == 0` — immediately `Ready(Err(\"limit\"))`. Otherwise decrement `left` and poll `inner`.","When `inner` returned `Pending`, it has already woken the task itself — just return `Pending`."]},{"t":"homework","title":"Async on tokio, the grown-up way","md":"Carry what you learned over to a real executor.\n\n1. Create a project and add tokio:\n```sh\ncargo new async-lab\ncd async-lab\ncargo add tokio --features full\n```\n2. Write a program that starts 100 tasks with `tokio::spawn`. Each sleeps a random time up to 50 ms (`tokio::time::sleep`) and sends its number into a `tokio::sync::mpsc` channel.\n3. The main task collects all the numbers and prints how many arrived and how many milliseconds it took.\n4. Add a `tokio::time::timeout` around the collecting: if not everything arrives within 1 second, print how many made it.\n5. Run it with `cargo run --release`.","check":["The program builds and prints `received 100`","The total time is close to 50 ms, not to 100 × 25 ms","With a 10 ms timeout, the program honestly prints how many made it","No `unwrap` on `send` without a comment explaining why it can't fail"]},{"t":"recap","items":["Your own Futures: `Ready`, `Join2`, `Limited` — all through `poll`.","A combinator polls the inner Futures and decides when it's ready.","In practice people use tokio, but understanding `poll` and the Waker helps fix hangs."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Write a few Futures and combinators of your own"]}]},{"id":"x05","title":"Your own iterator adapters","sub":"Wrapper adapters, double-ended iterators, tree traversal, from_fn, successors and itertools","lessons":[{"id":"x05-1","title":"Your own adapter","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"An adapter is a wrapper","md":"`map`, `filter`, `take` are **adapters**: structs that hold an inner iterator and call its `next` from their own `next`.\n\nYour own adapter is built the same way:\n1. A struct with an `inner: I` field, where `I: Iterator`.\n2. `impl Iterator` for it: `next` takes values from `inner` and does something with them.\n3. An extension trait, so you can write `.my_adapter()` on any iterator.\n\n> **Analogy:** a faucet attachment. Same water, but it comes out as a shower, a jet or drops."},{"t":"code","md":"An `every(n)` adapter: lets through every n-th item, starting with the first.","code":"struct Every<I> {\n    inner: I,\n    n: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<I: Iterator> Iterator for Every<I> {\n    type Item = I::Item;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {\n        let item = self.inner.next()?;\n        for _ in 1..self.n {\n            self.inner.next(); // skip n-1 items\n        }\n        Some(item)\n    }\n}\n\ntrait EveryExt: Iterator + Sized {\n    fn every(self, n: usize) -> Every<Self> {\n        assert!(n > 0, \"n must be greater than zero\");\n        Every { inner: self, n }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<I: Iterator> EveryExt for I {}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<i32> = (1..=10).every(3).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n    let s: String = \"abcdefg\".chars().every(2).collect();\n    println!(\"{s}\");\n}","out":"[1, 4, 7, 10]\naceg","after":"Our `every` works with **any** iterator: numbers, characters, anything."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does an adapter like `Filter` hold inside?","options":["The inner iterator and its own closure","All the items in advance","Just the number of items"],"answer":0,"why":["Right.","No, adapters are lazy and store nothing up front.","An adapter doesn't know the count in advance."]},{"t":"task","md":"Write a `Dedup` adapter: it skips consecutive equal items (like `uniq` in a terminal). Items must be `PartialEq + Clone`.","code":"struct Dedup<I: Iterator> {\n    inner: I,\n    last: Option<I::Item>,\n}\n\nimpl<I> Iterator for Dedup<I>\nwhere\n    I: Iterator,\n    I::Item: PartialEq + Clone,\n{\n    type Item = I::Item;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\ntrait DedupExt: Iterator + Sized {\n    fn dedup(self) -> Dedup<Self> {\n        Dedup { inner: self, last: None }\n    }\n}\nimpl<I: Iterator> DedupExt for I {}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<i32> = vec![1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 1, 1].into_iter().dedup().collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"struct Dedup<I: Iterator> {\n    inner: I,\n    last: Option<I::Item>,\n}\n\nimpl<I> Iterator for Dedup<I>\nwhere\n    I: Iterator,\n    I::Item: PartialEq + Clone,\n{\n    type Item = I::Item;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<I::Item> {\n        loop {\n            let x = self.inner.next()?;\n            if self.last.as_ref() != Some(&x) {\n                self.last = Some(x.clone());\n                return Some(x);\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}\n\ntrait DedupExt: Iterator + Sized {\n    fn dedup(self) -> Dedup<Self> {\n        Dedup { inner: self, last: None }\n    }\n}\nimpl<I: Iterator> DedupExt for I {}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<i32> = vec![1, 1, 2, 2, 2, 3, 1, 1].into_iter().dedup().collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"[1, 2, 3, 1]","hints":["In a loop, take the next item. If it isn't equal to `last`, remember a copy and return it.","Comparing `Option`s: `self.last.as_ref() != Some(&x)`."]},{"t":"task","md":"An `Interleave` adapter alternates the items of two iterators: `a1, b1, a2, b2...`. When one runs out, it continues with the other.","code":"struct Interleave<A, B> {\n    a: A,\n    b: B,\n    take_a: bool,\n}\n\nimpl<A, B> Iterator for Interleave<A, B>\nwhere\n    A: Iterator,\n    B: Iterator<Item = A::Item>,\n{\n    type Item = A::Item;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<A::Item> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn interleave<A, B>(a: A, b: B) -> Interleave<A, B> {\n    Interleave { a, b, take_a: true }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<i32> = interleave(vec![1, 3, 5, 7].into_iter(), vec![2, 4].into_iter()).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","solution":"struct Interleave<A, B> {\n    a: A,\n    b: B,\n    take_a: bool,\n}\n\nimpl<A, B> Iterator for Interleave<A, B>\nwhere\n    A: Iterator,\n    B: Iterator<Item = A::Item>,\n{\n    type Item = A::Item;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<A::Item> {\n        self.take_a = !self.take_a;\n        if !self.take_a {\n            self.a.next().or_else(|| self.b.next())\n        } else {\n            self.b.next().or_else(|| self.a.next())\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nfn interleave<A, B>(a: A, b: B) -> Interleave<A, B> {\n    Interleave { a, b, take_a: true }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let v: Vec<i32> = interleave(vec![1, 3, 5, 7].into_iter(), vec![2, 4].into_iter()).collect();\n    println!(\"{v:?}\");\n}","expect":"[1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 7]","hints":["Flip the flag on every step. If the iterator you need is empty — `or_else(|| other.next())`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Adapter = a struct with an inner iterator + `impl Iterator`.","An extension trait with a blanket impl gives `.my_adapter()` to every iterator.","Adapters are lazy: they work only while being pulled."]}],"goals":["Write an adapter — an iterator that wraps another iterator","Add it to every iterator through an extension trait"]},{"id":"x05-2","title":"Double-ended and exact-size iterators","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Extra iterator traits","md":"Besides `Iterator` there are \"superpower\" traits:\n- `DoubleEndedIterator` — can do `next_back()`: take from the end. Unlocks `rev()`, `rfind()`, a faster `last()`.\n- `ExactSizeIterator` — knows exactly how many are left: `len()`.\n- `FusedIterator` — after the first `None`, always `None`.\n\nStandard iterators (`Vec::iter`, ranges) have them all. Yours only if you implement them.\n\n> **Analogy:** a line you can join at either end. And a board saying \"5 people left\"."},{"t":"code","code":"struct Span {\n    lo: u32,\n    hi: u32, // exclusive\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Span {\n    type Item = u32;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {\n        if self.lo >= self.hi {\n            return None;\n        }\n        self.lo += 1;\n        Some(self.lo - 1)\n    }\n    fn size_hint(&self) -> (usize, Option<usize>) {\n        let n = (self.hi - self.lo) as usize;\n        (n, Some(n))\n    }\n}\n\nimpl DoubleEndedIterator for Span {\n    fn next_back(&mut self) -> Option<u32> {\n        if self.lo >= self.hi {\n            return None;\n        }\n        self.hi -= 1;\n        Some(self.hi)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl ExactSizeIterator for Span {}\n\nfn main() {\n    let back: Vec<u32> = Span { lo: 1, hi: 5 }.rev().collect();\n    println!(\"{back:?}\");\n    let mut s = Span { lo: 0, hi: 10 };\n    s.next();\n    s.next_back();\n    println!(\"{} left\", s.len());\n}","out":"[4, 3, 2, 1]\n8 left","after":"`ExactSizeIterator` is empty: `len()` comes from `size_hint`, which we made exact."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What do you implement to make `.rev()` work on your iterator?","options":["`ExactSizeIterator`","`DoubleEndedIterator` with a `next_back` method","Nothing, every iterator has `rev`"],"answer":1,"why":["That's for `len()`.","Right.","Only double-ended ones."]},{"t":"task","md":"The `Chars2` iterator walks the characters of a string. Implement `DoubleEndedIterator` for it so the palindrome check through `rev()` works.","code":"struct Chars2 {\n    chars: Vec<char>,\n    front: usize,\n    back: usize,\n}\n\nimpl Chars2 {\n    fn new(s: &str) -> Self {\n        let chars: Vec<char> = s.chars().collect();\n        let back = chars.len();\n        Chars2 { chars, front: 0, back }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Chars2 {\n    type Item = char;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<char> {\n        if self.front >= self.back {\n            return None;\n        }\n        self.front += 1;\n        Some(self.chars[self.front - 1])\n    }\n}\n\n// your code\n\nfn is_palindrome(s: &str) -> bool {\n    Chars2::new(s).eq(Chars2::new(s).rev())\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", is_palindrome(\"racecar\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", is_palindrome(\"crab\"));\n}","solution":"struct Chars2 {\n    chars: Vec<char>,\n    front: usize,\n    back: usize,\n}\n\nimpl Chars2 {\n    fn new(s: &str) -> Self {\n        let chars: Vec<char> = s.chars().collect();\n        let back = chars.len();\n        Chars2 { chars, front: 0, back }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Iterator for Chars2 {\n    type Item = char;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<char> {\n        if self.front >= self.back {\n            return None;\n        }\n        self.front += 1;\n        Some(self.chars[self.front - 1])\n    }\n}\n\nimpl DoubleEndedIterator for Chars2 {\n    fn next_back(&mut self) -> Option<char> {\n        if self.front >= self.back {\n            return None;\n        }\n        self.back -= 1;\n        Some(self.chars[self.back])\n    }\n}\n\nfn is_palindrome(s: &str) -> bool {\n    Chars2::new(s).eq(Chars2::new(s).rev())\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", is_palindrome(\"racecar\"));\n    println!(\"{}\", is_palindrome(\"crab\"));\n}","expect":"true\nfalse","hints":["`next_back` mirrors `next`: decrement `back` and return `chars[back]`."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`DoubleEndedIterator::next_back` — take from the end; `rev()` works.","`ExactSizeIterator` — an exact `len()`; it needs an exact `size_hint`.","A double-ended iterator keeps two \"cursors\": front and back."]}],"goals":["Implement `DoubleEndedIterator` so `rev()` works","Implement `ExactSizeIterator` so `len()` works"]},{"id":"x05-3","title":"Walking a tree with an iterator","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Turn recursion into a stack","md":"Walking a tree with a recursive function is easy. But an iterator can't work that way: `next` must return **one** value and remember where it stopped.\n\nThe fix is an **explicit stack**: it holds the nodes you still need to come back to. Each `next` pops a node, pushes its children and returns the value.\n\n> **Analogy:** bookmarks in a maze. You go down the left corridor and leave a marker at the fork, so you can come back for the right one."},{"t":"code","md":"Depth-first traversal (the node first, then the children left to right):","code":"struct Node {\n    name: &'static str,\n    children: Vec<Node>,\n}\n\nstruct DepthFirst<'a> {\n    stack: Vec<&'a Node>,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Iterator for DepthFirst<'a> {\n    type Item = &'a str;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a str> {\n        let node = self.stack.pop()?;\n        for child in node.children.iter().rev() {\n            self.stack.push(child); // rev — so the left child comes out first\n        }\n        Some(node.name)\n    }\n}\n\nimpl Node {\n    fn iter(&self) -> DepthFirst<'_> {\n        DepthFirst { stack: vec![self] }\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let tree = Node {\n        name: \"src\",\n        children: vec![\n            Node { name: \"main.rs\", children: vec![] },\n            Node { name: \"net\", children: vec![Node { name: \"tcp.rs\", children: vec![] }] },\n            Node { name: \"lib.rs\", children: vec![] },\n        ],\n    };\n    let all: Vec<&str> = tree.iter().collect();\n    println!(\"{all:?}\");\n    println!(\".rs files: {}\", tree.iter().filter(|n| n.ends_with(\".rs\")).count());\n}","out":"[\"src\", \"main.rs\", \"net\", \"tcp.rs\", \"lib.rs\"]\n.rs files: 3","after":"Since it's an ordinary `Iterator`, `filter`, `count` and everything else work right away."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why are the children pushed onto the stack in **reverse** order?","options":["A stack returns the last pushed item first; reverse order makes the left child come out first","To make the traversal faster","Without `rev` it won't compile"],"answer":0,"why":["Right.","The speed is the same.","It compiles, the order is just different."]},{"t":"task","md":"Make a **breadth-first** iterator (level by level): a `VecDeque` queue instead of a stack. For the same tree the order is `src, main.rs, net, lib.rs, tcp.rs`.","code":"use std::collections::VecDeque;\n\nstruct Node {\n    name: &'static str,\n    children: Vec<Node>,\n}\n\nstruct BreadthFirst<'a> {\n    queue: VecDeque<&'a Node>,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Iterator for BreadthFirst<'a> {\n    type Item = &'a str;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a str> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let tree = Node {\n        name: \"src\",\n        children: vec![\n            Node { name: \"main.rs\", children: vec![] },\n            Node { name: \"net\", children: vec![Node { name: \"tcp.rs\", children: vec![] }] },\n            Node { name: \"lib.rs\", children: vec![] },\n        ],\n    };\n    let it = BreadthFirst { queue: VecDeque::from([&tree]) };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", it.collect::<Vec<_>>());\n}","solution":"use std::collections::VecDeque;\n\nstruct Node {\n    name: &'static str,\n    children: Vec<Node>,\n}\n\nstruct BreadthFirst<'a> {\n    queue: VecDeque<&'a Node>,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Iterator for BreadthFirst<'a> {\n    type Item = &'a str;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<&'a str> {\n        let node = self.queue.pop_front()?;\n        self.queue.extend(node.children.iter());\n        Some(node.name)\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let tree = Node {\n        name: \"src\",\n        children: vec![\n            Node { name: \"main.rs\", children: vec![] },\n            Node { name: \"net\", children: vec![Node { name: \"tcp.rs\", children: vec![] }] },\n            Node { name: \"lib.rs\", children: vec![] },\n        ],\n    };\n    let it = BreadthFirst { queue: VecDeque::from([&tree]) };\n    println!(\"{:?}\", it.collect::<Vec<_>>());\n}","expect":"[\"src\", \"main.rs\", \"net\", \"lib.rs\", \"tcp.rs\"]","hints":["`pop_front` takes from the front of the queue, `extend(node.children.iter())` adds the children at the back."]},{"t":"recap","items":["A tree iterator — an explicit stack (depth-first) or a queue (breadth-first).","Each `next`: take a node, add its children, return the value.","The result is an ordinary iterator with every adapter."]}],"goals":["Write an iterator over a tree without recursion","Use an explicit stack"]},{"id":"x05-4","title":"from_fn, successors and itertools","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"An iterator from a closure","md":"You don't always need your own struct. The standard library has:\n- `std::iter::from_fn(|| ...)` — an iterator from a closure that returns an `Option`;\n- `std::iter::successors(first, |prev| next)` — each next item is computed from the previous one;\n- `std::iter::repeat_with(|| ...)` — call a closure forever."},{"t":"code","code":"fn main() {\n    // powers of two up to 64\n    let pows: Vec<u32> = std::iter::successors(Some(1u32), |&x| if x < 64 { Some(x * 2) } else { None }).collect();\n    println!(\"{pows:?}\");\n\n    // Collatz: from n until we reach 1\n    let collatz: Vec<u64> = std::iter::successors(Some(6u64), |&n| match n {\n        1 => None,\n        n if n % 2 == 0 => Some(n / 2),\n        n => Some(3 * n + 1),\n    })\n    .collect();\n    println!(\"{collatz:?}\");\n\n    // from_fn with its own state\n    let mut a = 0;\n    let mut b = 1;\n    let fib: Vec<u32> = std::iter::from_fn(|| {\n        let r = a;\n        (a, b) = (b, a + b);\n        Some(r)\n    })\n    .take(8)\n    .collect();\n    println!(\"{fib:?}\");\n}","out":"[1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64]\n[6, 3, 10, 5, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1]\n[0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13]"},{"t":"text","title":"itertools","md":"The **itertools** crate adds dozens of methods to iterators. The most useful:\n- `tuple_windows()` — windows as tuples: `(a, b)`, `(b, c)`;\n- `chunk_by(|x| key)` — groups of consecutive items with the same key;\n- `join(\", \")` — glue into a string;\n- `sorted_by_key(...)`, `unique()`, `counts()`, `cartesian_product(...)`.\n\nYou bring it in with one line: `use itertools::Itertools;`."},{"t":"code","crates":true,"code":"use itertools::Itertools;\n\nfn main() {\n    let temps = [3, 5, 4, 8, 10];\n    let rises = temps.iter().tuple_windows().filter(|(a, b)| b > a).count();\n    println!(\"it got warmer {rises} times\");\n\n    let words = [\"cat\", \"cow\", \"fox\", \"fig\", \"ox\"];\n    for (letter, group) in &words.iter().chunk_by(|w| w.chars().next().unwrap()) {\n        println!(\"{letter}: {}\", group.join(\", \"));\n    }\n\n    println!(\"{}\", (1..=3).cartesian_product(['a', 'b']).map(|(n, c)| format!(\"{n}{c}\")).join(\" \"));\n}","out":"it got warmer 3 times\nc: cat, cow\nf: fox, fig\no: ox\n1a 1b 2a 2b 3a 3b"},{"t":"quiz","md":"Which tool fits a sequence where \"each next item is computed from the previous one\" best?","options":["`repeat_with`","`successors`","`chain`"],"answer":1,"why":["It doesn't see the previous value.","Right.","That glues two iterators together."]},{"t":"task","md":"Use `from_fn` to make an iterator that reads numbers from the string `\"10 20 x 30\"` one by one and **stops** at the first non-number.","code":"fn main() {\n    let text = \"10 20 x 30\";\n    let mut parts = text.split(' ');\n    // your code: let nums: Vec<i32> = std::iter::from_fn(...).collect();\n    println!(\"{nums:?}\");\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let text = \"10 20 x 30\";\n    let mut parts = text.split(' ');\n    let nums: Vec<i32> = std::iter::from_fn(|| parts.next()?.parse().ok()).collect();\n    println!(\"{nums:?}\");\n}","expect":"[10, 20]","hints":["The closure: `|| parts.next()?.parse().ok()` — the first `None` stops the iterator."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`from_fn` — an iterator from a closure; `successors` — from the previous value.","itertools: `tuple_windows`, `chunk_by`, `join`, `cartesian_product`.","You need your own struct when you need `DoubleEnded` or complex state."]}],"goals":["Make iterators without your own struct","Know the most useful methods of the itertools crate"]},{"id":"x05-p","title":"Practice: iterators","min":25,"cards":[{"t":"task","md":"Run-length encoding: `\"aaabccdddd\"` → `[('a', 3), ('b', 1), ('c', 2), ('d', 4)]`. Use `peekable` or `fold`.","code":"fn rle(s: &str) -> Vec<(char, usize)> {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", rle(\"aaabccdddd\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", rle(\"\"));\n}","solution":"fn rle(s: &str) -> Vec<(char, usize)> {\n    let mut out: Vec<(char, usize)> = Vec::new();\n    for c in s.chars() {\n        match out.last_mut() {\n            Some((last, n)) if *last == c => *n += 1,\n            _ => out.push((c, 1)),\n        }\n    }\n    out\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{:?}\", rle(\"aaabccdddd\"));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", rle(\"\"));\n}","expect":"[('a', 3), ('b', 1), ('c', 2), ('d', 4)]\n[]","hints":["Look at the last element of the result through `last_mut()`. Same character — bump the counter, otherwise push `(c, 1)`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A `Batched` adapter collects items into a `Vec` of `n` (the last one may be shorter). Make an extension trait `.batched(n)`.","code":"struct Batched<I> {\n    inner: I,\n    n: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<I: Iterator> Iterator for Batched<I> {\n    type Item = Vec<I::Item>;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Vec<I::Item>> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\ntrait BatchedExt: Iterator + Sized {\n    fn batched(self, n: usize) -> Batched<Self> {\n        Batched { inner: self, n }\n    }\n}\nimpl<I: Iterator> BatchedExt for I {}\n\nfn main() {\n    for b in (1..=7).batched(3) {\n        println!(\"{b:?}\");\n    }\n}","solution":"struct Batched<I> {\n    inner: I,\n    n: usize,\n}\n\nimpl<I: Iterator> Iterator for Batched<I> {\n    type Item = Vec<I::Item>;\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<Vec<I::Item>> {\n        let batch: Vec<I::Item> = self.inner.by_ref().take(self.n).collect();\n        if batch.is_empty() { None } else { Some(batch) }\n    }\n}\n\ntrait BatchedExt: Iterator + Sized {\n    fn batched(self, n: usize) -> Batched<Self> {\n        Batched { inner: self, n }\n    }\n}\nimpl<I: Iterator> BatchedExt for I {}\n\nfn main() {\n    for b in (1..=7).batched(3) {\n        println!(\"{b:?}\");\n    }\n}","expect":"[1, 2, 3]\n[4, 5, 6]\n[7]","hints":["`self.inner.by_ref().take(self.n).collect()` — take up to `n` items without giving away the iterator itself.","An empty batch means everything has run out: `None`."]},{"t":"task","md":"A tree iterator with depth: yield `(depth, name)` pairs and print the tree with 2 spaces of indentation per level.","code":"struct Node {\n    name: &'static str,\n    children: Vec<Node>,\n}\n\nstruct WithDepth<'a> {\n    stack: Vec<(usize, &'a Node)>,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Iterator for WithDepth<'a> {\n    type Item = (usize, &'a str);\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<(usize, &'a str)> {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let tree = Node {\n        name: \"root\",\n        children: vec![\n            Node { name: \"a\", children: vec![Node { name: \"a1\", children: vec![] }] },\n            Node { name: \"b\", children: vec![] },\n        ],\n    };\n    for (d, name) in (WithDepth { stack: vec![(0, &tree)] }) {\n        println!(\"{}{name}\", \"  \".repeat(d));\n    }\n}","solution":"struct Node {\n    name: &'static str,\n    children: Vec<Node>,\n}\n\nstruct WithDepth<'a> {\n    stack: Vec<(usize, &'a Node)>,\n}\n\nimpl<'a> Iterator for WithDepth<'a> {\n    type Item = (usize, &'a str);\n    fn next(&mut self) -> Option<(usize, &'a str)> {\n        let (d, node) = self.stack.pop()?;\n        for child in node.children.iter().rev() {\n            self.stack.push((d + 1, child));\n        }\n        Some((d, node.name))\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let tree = Node {\n        name: \"root\",\n        children: vec![\n            Node { name: \"a\", children: vec![Node { name: \"a1\", children: vec![] }] },\n            Node { name: \"b\", children: vec![] },\n        ],\n    };\n    for (d, name) in (WithDepth { stack: vec![(0, &tree)] }) {\n        println!(\"{}{name}\", \"  \".repeat(d));\n    }\n}","expect":"root\n  a\n    a1\n  b","hints":["Keep `(depth, node)` pairs on the stack. Children get depth `d + 1`."]},{"t":"task","md":"Using `successors`, find how many steps the Collatz sequence from 27 takes to reach 1, and the largest number along the way.","code":"fn main() {\n    // your code\n}","solution":"fn main() {\n    let seq: Vec<u64> = std::iter::successors(Some(27u64), |&n| match n {\n        1 => None,\n        n if n % 2 == 0 => Some(n / 2),\n        n => Some(3 * n + 1),\n    })\n    .collect();\n    println!(\"steps: {}\", seq.len() - 1);\n    println!(\"max: {}\", seq.iter().max().unwrap());\n}","expect":"steps: 111\nmax: 9232","hints":["The number of steps is the length of the sequence minus 1 (the start itself isn't a step)."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`last_mut()` — handy for grouping consecutive items.","`by_ref().take(n)` — take a piece without giving away the iterator.","A stack with depth — a tree with indentation.","`successors` — sequences \"from the previous one\"."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Solve tasks on your own adapters and generators"]}]},{"id":"x06","title":"Memory and allocators","sub":"Size and alignment, your own global allocator, arenas and small tricks","lessons":[{"id":"x06-1","title":"Size, alignment and Layout","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Alignment","md":"The processor reads a number faster when its address is a **multiple** of its size: a `u32` from an address divisible by 4, a `u64` — by 8. This is **alignment**.\n\nSo empty bytes are sometimes inserted between struct fields — **padding**. Rust reorders fields by itself to reduce padding. With `#[repr(C)]` the order stays as written — the C language requires that.\n\n> **Analogy:** boxes on a shelf that can only stand on marks every 4 cm. A small box between big ones sometimes has to stand with a gap."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::mem::{align_of, size_of};\n\n#[allow(dead_code)]\nstruct Rusty {\n    a: u8,\n    b: u32,\n    c: u8,\n}\n\n#[allow(dead_code)]\n#[repr(C)]\nstruct LikeC {\n    a: u8,\n    b: u32,\n    c: u8,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"u8: {} / {}\", size_of::<u8>(), align_of::<u8>());\n    println!(\"u32: {} / {}\", size_of::<u32>(), align_of::<u32>());\n    println!(\"Rusty: {}\", size_of::<Rusty>());\n    println!(\"LikeC: {}\", size_of::<LikeC>());\n}","out":"u8: 1 / 1\nu32: 4 / 4\nRusty: 8\nLikeC: 12","after":"Same fields, different sizes. In `LikeC` there are 3 bytes of padding after `a` and 3 more after `c`. In `Rusty`, Rust put `b` first."},{"t":"text","title":"Layout","md":"`std::alloc::Layout` is a pair \"size + alignment\". That's exactly what an allocator needs to know:\n\n```\nuse std::alloc::Layout;\nlet l = Layout::new::<u64>();          // 8 bytes, alignment 8\nlet arr = Layout::array::<u16>(10);    // 20 bytes, alignment 2\n```\nIt'll come in handy in the next lessons, when we allocate memory by hand."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why can a `#[repr(C)]` struct be bigger than a normal one?","options":["Rust doesn't reorder its fields, so there's more padding","`repr(C)` adds a service header","The fields become 64-bit"],"answer":0,"why":["Right.","There are no headers.","Field types don't change."]},{"t":"task","md":"Predict and check: print the sizes of three types: `(u8, u64)`, `[u16; 3]` and `Option<u32>`.","code":"use std::mem::size_of;\n\nfn main() {\n    // your code\n}","solution":"use std::mem::size_of;\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{}\", size_of::<(u8, u64)>());\n    println!(\"{}\", size_of::<[u16; 3]>());\n    println!(\"{}\", size_of::<Option<u32>>());\n}","expect":"16\n6\n8","hints":["`size_of::<Type>()`. The tuple `(u8, u64)` has alignment 8, so the `u8` takes a full 8 bytes with padding.","`Option<u32>` has to store \"present or not\" somewhere — hence 8, not 4."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Alignment: the address is a multiple of the size. There can be padding between fields.","Rust reorders fields itself; `#[repr(C)]` keeps the C order.","`Layout` = size + alignment."]}],"goals":["See why a struct can be bigger than the sum of its fields","Find size and alignment with `size_of` and `align_of`"]},{"id":"x06-2","title":"Your own global allocator","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Who hands out memory","md":"When a `Vec` or a `Box` wants heap memory, they call the **global allocator**. By default it's the system one (`malloc` on Linux, `HeapAlloc` on Windows).\n\nYou can replace it with your own: implement the `GlobalAlloc` trait and mark a static variable with `#[global_allocator]`. That's how fast allocators (`mimalloc`, `jemalloc`) are plugged in, or allocations are counted.\n\n> **Analogy:** a warehouse that hands out boxes to the whole factory. You can put your own clerk at the gate who writes down who took how much and passes the request on to the real warehouse."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::alloc::{GlobalAlloc, Layout, System};\nuse std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};\n\nstruct Counting;\n\nstatic ALLOCS: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);\nstatic BYTES: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);\n\nunsafe impl GlobalAlloc for Counting {\n    unsafe fn alloc(&self, layout: Layout) -> *mut u8 {\n        ALLOCS.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);\n        BYTES.fetch_add(layout.size(), Ordering::Relaxed);\n        unsafe { System.alloc(layout) }\n    }\n    unsafe fn dealloc(&self, ptr: *mut u8, layout: Layout) {\n        unsafe { System.dealloc(ptr, layout) }\n    }\n}\n\n#[global_allocator]\nstatic GLOBAL: Counting = Counting;\n\nfn measure(f: impl FnOnce()) -> (usize, usize) {\n    let (a0, b0) = (ALLOCS.load(Ordering::Relaxed), BYTES.load(Ordering::Relaxed));\n    f();\n    (ALLOCS.load(Ordering::Relaxed) - a0, BYTES.load(Ordering::Relaxed) - b0)\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let grow = measure(|| {\n        let mut v = Vec::new();\n        for i in 0..100u32 {\n            v.push(i);\n        }\n    });\n    let once = measure(|| {\n        let mut v = Vec::with_capacity(100);\n        for i in 0..100u32 {\n            v.push(i);\n        }\n    });\n    println!(\"push one by one: {} allocations\", grow.0);\n    println!(\"with_capacity: {} allocation, {} bytes\", once.0, once.1);\n}","out":"push one by one: 6 allocations\nwith_capacity: 1 allocation, 400 bytes","after":"The measurement confirms the Level 2 speed module: `with_capacity` — one allocation instead of six."},{"t":"text","title":"Why unsafe","md":"`GlobalAlloc` is an `unsafe` trait: the compiler can't check that the allocator hands out correct memory. A mistake here breaks the **whole** program. That's why a custom allocator usually just **wraps** the system one, like ours."},{"t":"quiz","md":"What does the `#[global_allocator]` attribute do?","options":["Makes the program faster","Says \"the whole program gets memory through this allocator\"","Turns the heap off"],"answer":1,"why":["Not by itself.","Right.","The heap stays; only who hands it out changes."]},{"t":"task","md":"Using the ready counting allocator, find out how many allocations `format!(\"{}-{}\", a, b)` makes and how many `String::with_capacity` + two `push_str` make. Print both numbers.","code":"use std::alloc::{GlobalAlloc, Layout, System};\nuse std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};\n\nstruct Counting;\nstatic ALLOCS: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);\n\nunsafe impl GlobalAlloc for Counting {\n    unsafe fn alloc(&self, layout: Layout) -> *mut u8 {\n        ALLOCS.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);\n        unsafe { System.alloc(layout) }\n    }\n    unsafe fn dealloc(&self, ptr: *mut u8, layout: Layout) {\n        unsafe { System.dealloc(ptr, layout) }\n    }\n}\n\n#[global_allocator]\nstatic GLOBAL: Counting = Counting;\n\nfn count(f: impl FnOnce()) -> usize {\n    let a0 = ALLOCS.load(Ordering::Relaxed);\n    f();\n    ALLOCS.load(Ordering::Relaxed) - a0\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = \"привет\";\n    let b = \"мир\";\n    // your code: two measurements and a print\n}","solution":"use std::alloc::{GlobalAlloc, Layout, System};\nuse std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};\n\nstruct Counting;\nstatic ALLOCS: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);\n\nunsafe impl GlobalAlloc for Counting {\n    unsafe fn alloc(&self, layout: Layout) -> *mut u8 {\n        ALLOCS.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);\n        unsafe { System.alloc(layout) }\n    }\n    unsafe fn dealloc(&self, ptr: *mut u8, layout: Layout) {\n        unsafe { System.dealloc(ptr, layout) }\n    }\n}\n\n#[global_allocator]\nstatic GLOBAL: Counting = Counting;\n\nfn count(f: impl FnOnce()) -> usize {\n    let a0 = ALLOCS.load(Ordering::Relaxed);\n    f();\n    ALLOCS.load(Ordering::Relaxed) - a0\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let a = \"привет\";\n    let b = \"мир\";\n    let with_format = count(|| {\n        let s = format!(\"{}-{}\", a, b);\n        std::hint::black_box(s);\n    });\n    let with_cap = count(|| {\n        let mut s = String::with_capacity(a.len() + b.len() + 1);\n        s.push_str(a);\n        s.push('-');\n        s.push_str(b);\n        std::hint::black_box(s);\n    });\n    println!(\"format!: {with_format}\");\n    println!(\"with_capacity: {with_cap}\");\n}","expect":"format!: 2\nwith_capacity: 1","hints":["Wrap each variant in `count(|| { ... })`.","`black_box(s)` keeps the compiler from throwing the string away as unused.","`format!` estimates the length in advance, but with multi-byte text and insertions it often guesses low and allocates a second time. An exact capacity — just once. Measuring beats guessing."]},{"t":"recap","items":["`GlobalAlloc` + `#[global_allocator]` — your own allocator for the whole program.","Most often it wraps `System` and counts something.","Measure first, then optimize — measurements can surprise you."]}],"goals":["Replace the allocator of the whole program","Count how many times the program asks for memory"]},{"id":"x06-3","title":"Arenas","min":9,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"Why an arena","md":"Sometimes a program creates **lots of small objects** with the same lifetime: nodes of a parse tree, the objects of one game frame, the data of one request. Allocating each one separately is expensive. Freeing them one by one is too.\n\nAn **arena** allocates a big chunk and hands it out in pieces. Everything is freed **at once** when the arena dies.\n\n> **Analogy:** a disposable tablecloth at a picnic. You don't wash every plate — at the end you roll everything up together."},{"t":"text","title":"An index-based arena","md":"The simplest and safest arena: all objects live in one `Vec`, and **indexes** replace references. Indexes don't get in the way of borrow checking, and you can build graphs and trees with links in both directions."},{"t":"code","code":"#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq)]\nstruct NodeId(usize);\n\nstruct Node {\n    value: i32,\n    parent: Option<NodeId>,\n    children: Vec<NodeId>,\n}\n\nstruct Tree {\n    nodes: Vec<Node>,\n}\n\nimpl Tree {\n    fn add(&mut self, value: i32, parent: Option<NodeId>) -> NodeId {\n        let id = NodeId(self.nodes.len());\n        self.nodes.push(Node { value, parent, children: vec![] });\n        if let Some(p) = parent {\n            self.nodes[p.0].children.push(id);\n        }\n        id\n    }\n    fn path_to_root(&self, mut id: NodeId) -> Vec<i32> {\n        let mut path = vec![self.nodes[id.0].value];\n        while let Some(p) = self.nodes[id.0].parent {\n            path.push(self.nodes[p.0].value);\n            id = p;\n        }\n        path\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut t = Tree { nodes: vec![] };\n    let root = t.add(1, None);\n    let a = t.add(2, Some(root));\n    let b = t.add(3, Some(a));\n    println!(\"{:?}\", t.path_to_root(b));\n    println!(\"root's children: {}\", t.nodes[root.0].children.len());\n}","out":"[3, 2, 1]\nroot's children: 1","after":"A link \"up\" to the parent without `Rc`, `Weak` and `RefCell` — just a number."},{"t":"code","crates":true,"md":"**bumpalo** is a real bump allocator: it allocates from a big chunk by simply moving a pointer. Very fast.","code":"use bumpalo::Bump;\n\nfn main() {\n    let bump = Bump::new();\n    let x: &mut u32 = bump.alloc(10);\n    *x += 5;\n    let name: &str = bump.alloc_str(\"crab\");\n    let nums: &[i32] = bump.alloc_slice_copy(&[1, 2, 3]);\n    println!(\"{x} {name} {nums:?}\");\n    println!(\"allocated in the arena: at least that many bytes {}\", bump.allocated_bytes() >= 4 + 4 + 12);\n}","out":"15 crab [1, 2, 3]\nallocated in the arena: at least that many bytes true","after":"All references live as long as `bump` lives. When the arena goes, everything is freed at once."},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why is an index-based arena handy for trees with a link to the parent?","options":["An index is just a number; it doesn't get in the way of borrow checking","Indexes are faster than pointers","The arena drops nodes one by one"],"answer":0,"why":["Right.","The speed is similar; the point is simplicity.","The opposite: all at once."]},{"t":"task","md":"Add a `depth(id)` method to the arena tree that returns a node's depth (the root is 0), and a `sum_subtree(id)` method that adds up the values of a node and all its descendants.","code":"#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\nstruct NodeId(usize);\n\nstruct Node {\n    value: i32,\n    parent: Option<NodeId>,\n    children: Vec<NodeId>,\n}\n\nstruct Tree {\n    nodes: Vec<Node>,\n}\n\nimpl Tree {\n    fn add(&mut self, value: i32, parent: Option<NodeId>) -> NodeId {\n        let id = NodeId(self.nodes.len());\n        self.nodes.push(Node { value, parent, children: vec![] });\n        if let Some(p) = parent {\n            self.nodes[p.0].children.push(id);\n        }\n        id\n    }\n    // your code: depth and sum_subtree\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut t = Tree { nodes: vec![] };\n    let root = t.add(1, None);\n    let a = t.add(2, Some(root));\n    let _b = t.add(3, Some(root));\n    let c = t.add(4, Some(a));\n    println!(\"{}\", t.depth(c));\n    println!(\"{}\", t.sum_subtree(root));\n    println!(\"{}\", t.sum_subtree(a));\n}","solution":"#[derive(Clone, Copy)]\nstruct NodeId(usize);\n\nstruct Node {\n    value: i32,\n    parent: Option<NodeId>,\n    children: Vec<NodeId>,\n}\n\nstruct Tree {\n    nodes: Vec<Node>,\n}\n\nimpl Tree {\n    fn add(&mut self, value: i32, parent: Option<NodeId>) -> NodeId {\n        let id = NodeId(self.nodes.len());\n        self.nodes.push(Node { value, parent, children: vec![] });\n        if let Some(p) = parent {\n            self.nodes[p.0].children.push(id);\n        }\n        id\n    }\n    fn depth(&self, mut id: NodeId) -> usize {\n        let mut d = 0;\n        while let Some(p) = self.nodes[id.0].parent {\n            d += 1;\n            id = p;\n        }\n        d\n    }\n    fn sum_subtree(&self, id: NodeId) -> i32 {\n        let node = &self.nodes[id.0];\n        node.value + node.children.iter().map(|&c| self.sum_subtree(c)).sum::<i32>()\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut t = Tree { nodes: vec![] };\n    let root = t.add(1, None);\n    let a = t.add(2, Some(root));\n    let _b = t.add(3, Some(root));\n    let c = t.add(4, Some(a));\n    println!(\"{}\", t.depth(c));\n    println!(\"{}\", t.sum_subtree(root));\n    println!(\"{}\", t.sum_subtree(a));\n}","expect":"2\n10\n6","hints":["`depth` — walk to the parent while there is one, counting steps.","`sum_subtree` — the node's value plus the sum of `sum_subtree` over its children (recursion)."]},{"t":"recap","items":["An arena: allocate many small things, free everything at once.","An index-based arena — a `Vec` + `struct Id(usize)` instead of references.","`bumpalo` — a fast bump allocator with ordinary references."]}],"goals":["Understand the arena idea: allocate a lot, free everything at once","Make an index-based arena and try the bumpalo crate"]},{"id":"x06-4","title":"Small memory tricks","min":8,"cards":[{"t":"text","title":"A niche: Option for free","md":"`Option<Box<T>>` takes **as much** space as `Box<T>`. Why? A `Box` is never a null pointer — so zero can be used for `None`. This is called the **niche optimization**.\n\n`Option<&T>`, `Option<NonZeroU32>`, `Option<Vec<T>>` are free in the same way."},{"t":"code","code":"use std::mem::size_of;\nuse std::num::NonZeroU32;\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{} {}\", size_of::<Box<u64>>(), size_of::<Option<Box<u64>>>());\n    println!(\"{} {}\", size_of::<u32>(), size_of::<Option<u32>>());\n    println!(\"{} {}\", size_of::<NonZeroU32>(), size_of::<Option<NonZeroU32>>());\n    println!(\"{} {}\", size_of::<Vec<u8>>(), size_of::<Box<[u8]>>());\n}","out":"8 8\n4 8\n4 4\n24 16","after":"- `Option<u32>` got bigger: a `u32` has no \"forbidden\" values.\n- `NonZeroU32` is never zero — and `Option` is free again.\n- `Box<[u8]>` is 8 bytes smaller than `Vec<u8>`: it needs no capacity, only a length."},{"t":"text","title":"Data on the stack","md":"Small collections can live **without the heap**:\n- `[T; N]` — a fixed-size array;\n- the `arrayvec::ArrayVec<T, N>` crate — like `Vec`, but at most `N` items, all on the stack;\n- the `smallvec::SmallVec<[T; N]>` crate — up to `N` items on the stack; more moves to the heap.\n\nUseful when there are millions of collections, each usually holding 2–3 items."},{"t":"code","crates":true,"code":"use smallvec::SmallVec;\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut v: SmallVec<[u32; 4]> = SmallVec::new();\n    for i in 0..4 {\n        v.push(i);\n    }\n    println!(\"{:?} on the heap? {}\", v, v.spilled());\n    v.push(99);\n    println!(\"{} items, on the heap? {}\", v.len(), v.spilled());\n}","out":"[0, 1, 2, 3] on the heap? false\n5 items, on the heap? true"},{"t":"quiz","md":"Why isn't `Option<&T>` bigger than `&T`?","options":["A reference is never null, and null is used for `None`","`Option` weighs nothing at all","The compiler stores `None` in a separate table"],"answer":0,"why":["Right. That's a niche.","`Option<u32>` weighs more than `u32`.","There's no table."]},{"t":"task","md":"The `freeze` function turns a `Vec<String>` into a `Box<[String]>` — an immutable list without extra capacity. Print the length and the difference between the two \"headers\" via `size_of_val`.","code":"use std::mem::size_of_val;\n\nfn freeze(v: Vec<String>) -> Box<[String]> {\n    // your code\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut v = Vec::with_capacity(100);\n    v.push(String::from(\"a\"));\n    v.push(String::from(\"b\"));\n    let header_vec = size_of_val(&v);\n    let frozen = freeze(v);\n    println!(\"{} {}\", frozen.len(), header_vec - size_of_val(&frozen));\n}","solution":"use std::mem::size_of_val;\n\nfn freeze(v: Vec<String>) -> Box<[String]> {\n    v.into_boxed_slice()\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut v = Vec::with_capacity(100);\n    v.push(String::from(\"a\"));\n    v.push(String::from(\"b\"));\n    let header_vec = size_of_val(&v);\n    let frozen = freeze(v);\n    println!(\"{} {}\", frozen.len(), header_vec - size_of_val(&frozen));\n}","expect":"2 8","hints":["`Vec` has an `into_boxed_slice()` method. It also gives back the extra capacity."]},{"t":"recap","items":["Niche: `Option<Box<T>>`, `Option<&T>`, `Option<NonZero*>` are free.","`Box<[T]>` — a list without capacity; its header is 8 bytes smaller.","`SmallVec`, `ArrayVec` — small collections on the stack."]}],"goals":["Learn about the \"niche\" optimization","Save the heap: data on the stack and `Box<[T]>`"]},{"id":"x06-p","title":"Practice: memory","min":25,"cards":[{"t":"task","outputCheck":"number","md":"A counting allocator + a comparison: build a string of 1000 numbers with `write!` into `String::new()` and into `String::with_capacity(4000)`. Print how many times fewer allocations the second way makes (integer division).","code":"use std::alloc::{GlobalAlloc, Layout, System};\nuse std::fmt::Write;\nuse std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};\n\nstruct Counting;\nstatic ALLOCS: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);\n\nunsafe impl GlobalAlloc for Counting {\n    unsafe fn alloc(&self, layout: Layout) -> *mut u8 {\n        ALLOCS.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);\n        unsafe { System.alloc(layout) }\n    }\n    unsafe fn dealloc(&self, ptr: *mut u8, layout: Layout) {\n        unsafe { System.dealloc(ptr, layout) }\n    }\n    unsafe fn realloc(&self, ptr: *mut u8, layout: Layout, new_size: usize) -> *mut u8 {\n        ALLOCS.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);\n        unsafe { System.realloc(ptr, layout, new_size) }\n    }\n}\n\n#[global_allocator]\nstatic GLOBAL: Counting = Counting;\n\nfn count(f: impl FnOnce()) -> usize {\n    let a0 = ALLOCS.load(Ordering::Relaxed);\n    f();\n    ALLOCS.load(Ordering::Relaxed) - a0\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    // your code\n}","solution":"use std::alloc::{GlobalAlloc, Layout, System};\nuse std::fmt::Write;\nuse std::sync::atomic::{AtomicUsize, Ordering};\n\nstruct Counting;\nstatic ALLOCS: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);\n\nunsafe impl GlobalAlloc for Counting {\n    unsafe fn alloc(&self, layout: Layout) -> *mut u8 {\n        ALLOCS.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);\n        unsafe { System.alloc(layout) }\n    }\n    unsafe fn dealloc(&self, ptr: *mut u8, layout: Layout) {\n        unsafe { System.dealloc(ptr, layout) }\n    }\n    unsafe fn realloc(&self, ptr: *mut u8, layout: Layout, new_size: usize) -> *mut u8 {\n        ALLOCS.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed);\n        unsafe { System.realloc(ptr, layout, new_size) }\n    }\n}\n\n#[global_allocator]\nstatic GLOBAL: Counting = Counting;\n\nfn count(f: impl FnOnce()) -> usize {\n    let a0 = ALLOCS.load(Ordering::Relaxed);\n    f();\n    ALLOCS.load(Ordering::Relaxed) - a0\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let slow = count(|| {\n        let mut s = String::new();\n        for i in 0..1000 {\n            write!(s, \"{i}\").unwrap();\n        }\n        std::hint::black_box(s);\n    });\n    let fast = count(|| {\n        let mut s = String::with_capacity(4000);\n        for i in 0..1000 {\n            write!(s, \"{i}\").unwrap();\n        }\n        std::hint::black_box(s);\n    });\n    println!(\"{}\", slow / fast);\n}","expect":"10","hints":["Write the numbers into the string with `write!(s, \"{i}\")` — you need `use std::fmt::Write`.","`realloc` counts too: as the string grows, it \"moves\" through it."]},{"t":"task","md":"A string arena: `Interner` stores each distinct string once and hands out its number. A repeated string gets the same number. Write `intern` and `lookup`.","code":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nstruct Interner {\n    map: HashMap<String, u32>,\n    names: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nimpl Interner {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        Interner { map: HashMap::new(), names: Vec::new() }\n    }\n    fn intern(&mut self, s: &str) -> u32 {\n        // your code\n    }\n    fn lookup(&self, id: u32) -> &str {\n        // your code\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut i = Interner::new();\n    let ids: Vec<u32> = [\"cat\", \"dog\", \"cat\", \"ox\", \"dog\"].iter().map(|s| i.intern(s)).collect();\n    println!(\"{ids:?}\");\n    println!(\"{} {}\", i.lookup(2), i.names.len());\n}","solution":"use std::collections::HashMap;\n\nstruct Interner {\n    map: HashMap<String, u32>,\n    names: Vec<String>,\n}\n\nimpl Interner {\n    fn new() -> Self {\n        Interner { map: HashMap::new(), names: Vec::new() }\n    }\n    fn intern(&mut self, s: &str) -> u32 {\n        if let Some(&id) = self.map.get(s) {\n            return id;\n        }\n        let id = self.names.len() as u32;\n        self.names.push(s.to_string());\n        self.map.insert(s.to_string(), id);\n        id\n    }\n    fn lookup(&self, id: u32) -> &str {\n        &self.names[id as usize]\n    }\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    let mut i = Interner::new();\n    let ids: Vec<u32> = [\"cat\", \"dog\", \"cat\", \"ox\", \"dog\"].iter().map(|s| i.intern(s)).collect();\n    println!(\"{ids:?}\");\n    println!(\"{} {}\", i.lookup(2), i.names.len());\n}","expect":"[0, 1, 0, 2, 1]\nox 3","hints":["In the map — return the number. Not there — the number is the length of `names`; add the string to both places."]},{"t":"task","md":"A compact record: the struct `Pixel { visible: bool, x: u16, color: u32, y: u16 }`. Compare its size with a `#[repr(C)]` version with the same field order and print both numbers.","code":"use std::mem::size_of;\n\n#[allow(dead_code)]\nstruct Pixel {\n    visible: bool,\n    x: u16,\n    color: u32,\n    y: u16,\n}\n\n// your code: PixelC with #[repr(C)]\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{} {}\", size_of::<Pixel>(), size_of::<PixelC>());\n}","solution":"use std::mem::size_of;\n\n#[allow(dead_code)]\nstruct Pixel {\n    visible: bool,\n    x: u16,\n    color: u32,\n    y: u16,\n}\n\n#[allow(dead_code)]\n#[repr(C)]\nstruct PixelC {\n    visible: bool,\n    x: u16,\n    color: u32,\n    y: u16,\n}\n\nfn main() {\n    println!(\"{} {}\", size_of::<Pixel>(), size_of::<PixelC>());\n}","expect":"12 12","hints":["Copy the struct and add `#[repr(C)]`.","A surprise: the sizes are equal. In C: `bool` + 1 byte of padding + `u16` + `u32` + `u16` + 2 trailing bytes = 12. Rust will reorder the fields, but 9 bytes still round up to 12 because of the `u32` alignment."]},{"t":"homework","title":"Profiling memory","md":"Look at the allocations of a real program.\n\n1. Take any project of yours (or the Level 2 final project) and add the `dhat` crate:\n```sh\ncargo add dhat\n```\n2. Following the `dhat` documentation, plug it in as the global allocator behind a `dhat-heap` feature.\n3. Run: `cargo run --release --features dhat-heap`. A `dhat-heap.json` file appears.\n4. Open it in the **DHAT Viewer** (the dh_view page from the Valgrind docs) and find the place with the most allocations.\n5. Try to reduce them: `with_capacity`, `&str` instead of `String`, reusing a buffer.","check":["`dhat-heap.json` is created after the run","The place with the most allocations is found","After the fix the allocation count went down, and the new report shows it"]},{"t":"recap","items":["A counting allocator is a quick way to measure allocations.","String interning — an arena with lookup by value.","Sometimes reordering fields gains nothing — check, don't guess."]}],"practice":true,"goals":["Measure and reduce work with memory"]}]}],"glossary":[]};
